{"type":"custom","customType":"web-search-results","data":{"id":"mp8ahs8wzm7ei6","type":"fetch","timestamp":1778932523840,"urls":[{"url":"https://www.mustier.ai/projects/pi-extensions","title":"Pi Extensions","content":"## Overview\n\nA collection of extensions for [Pi](https://pi.dev), a coding agent harness. These extensions add capabilities that help manage long-running tasks and improve the developer experience.\n\n## Extensions\n\n### Slash commands\n\n*   [/files](/projects/files) – in-terminal file browser with diffs and line selection\n*   [/usage](/projects/pi-usage) – get your Pi metrics per provider + model\n*   [/code](/projects/pi-code) – copy/insert code snippets from your agent\n*   [/raw-paste](/projects/pi-raw-paste) – paste editable text instead of `[paste #1 +21 lines]`\n\n### [/ralph-wiggum](/projects/ralph-wiggum)\n\nRun arbitrarily-long tasks without diluting model attention. A \"flat\" iteration loop without subagents – the model works through a checklist, reflecting periodically.\n\n### [arcade](/projects/pi-arcade)\n\nBecause sometimes you need a break:\n\n*   👾 sPIce-invaders\n*   👻 picman\n*   🏓 ping\n*   🧩 tetris\n*   🍄 not-mario\n\nAll in ASCII, all in your terminal.\n\n### [pi-nes](/projects/pi-nes)\n\nBecause I can. A full NES emulator running inside Pi with a Rust core and Kitty graphics support.\n\n### [tab-status](/projects/pi-tab-status)\n\nTerminal tab indicators showing session state at a glance:\n\n*   ✅ Done\n*   🚧 Stuck / needs input\n*   🛑 Timed out\n\nHelps manage multiple parallel agent sessions. Currently set up for one session/tab.\n\n## Installation\n\nFirst, install Pi:\n\n```\nnpm install -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\n```\n\nThen install individual extensions via npm:\n\n```\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-files-widget\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-usage-extension\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-code-actions\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-raw-paste\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-ralph-wiggum\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-arcade\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-tab-status\n```\n\nOr install the full collection via git:\n\n```\npi install git:github.com/tmustier/pi-extensions\n```\n\n## Why?\n\nPi is great out of the box, but I wanted better task management for long sessions and quality-of-life improvements like tab indicators and quick code insertion.","error":null}]},"id":"907c2711","parentId":"d52e2292","timestamp":"2026-05-16T11:55:23.840Z"}
{"type":"message","id":"9edae358","parentId":"907c2711","timestamp":"2026-05-16T11:55:23.840Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_NEK4tcnWklNW1kfNbZ58NnfA|fc_02f887b473d5faa9016a085b20a6ec81908f7c594ce9e71444","toolName":"fetch_content","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Overview\n\nA collection of extensions for [Pi](https://pi.dev), a coding agent harness. These extensions add capabilities that help manage long-running tasks and improve the developer experience.\n\n## Extensions\n\n### Slash commands\n\n*   [/files](/projects/files) – in-terminal file browser with diffs and line selection\n*   [/usage](/projects/pi-usage) – get your Pi metrics per provider + model\n*   [/code](/projects/pi-code) – copy/insert code snippets from your agent\n*   [/raw-paste](/projects/pi-raw-paste) – paste editable text instead of `[paste #1 +21 lines]`\n\n### [/ralph-wiggum](/projects/ralph-wiggum)\n\nRun arbitrarily-long tasks without diluting model attention. A \"flat\" iteration loop without subagents – the model works through a checklist, reflecting periodically.\n\n### [arcade](/projects/pi-arcade)\n\nBecause sometimes you need a break:\n\n*   👾 sPIce-invaders\n*   👻 picman\n*   🏓 ping\n*   🧩 tetris\n*   🍄 not-mario\n\nAll in ASCII, all in your terminal.\n\n### [pi-nes](/projects/pi-nes)\n\nBecause I can. A full NES emulator running inside Pi with a Rust core and Kitty graphics support.\n\n### [tab-status](/projects/pi-tab-status)\n\nTerminal tab indicators showing session state at a glance:\n\n*   ✅ Done\n*   🚧 Stuck / needs input\n*   🛑 Timed out\n\nHelps manage multiple parallel agent sessions. Currently set up for one session/tab.\n\n## Installation\n\nFirst, install Pi:\n\n```\nnpm install -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\n```\n\nThen install individual extensions via npm:\n\n```\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-files-widget\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-usage-extension\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-code-actions\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-raw-paste\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-ralph-wiggum\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-arcade\nnpm install @tmustier/pi-tab-status\n```\n\nOr install the full collection via git:\n\n```\npi install git:github.com/tmustier/pi-extensions\n```\n\n## Why?\n\nPi is great out of the box, but I wanted better task management for long sessions and quality-of-life improvements like tab indicators and quick code insertion."}],"details":{"urls":["https://www.mustier.ai/projects/pi-extensions"],"urlCount":1,"successful":1,"totalChars":2021,"title":"Pi Extensions","responseId":"mp8ahs8wzm7ei6","truncated":false,"hasImage":false,"imageCount":0,"prompt":"Compare this Pi extensions project/page to LazyPi as a way to discover/install/use Pi extensions. Focus on what it offers, overlap, differences, and recommendation for an existing Pi user."},"isError":false,"timestamp":1778932523840}}
{"type":"message","id":"97cf2069","parentId":"9edae358","timestamp":"2026-05-16T11:55:37.689Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"They’re different things:\n\n| Project | What it is | Best for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **LazyPi** | Curated installer/bootstrapper for a broad Pi setup | “Give me a full opinionated Pi distribution” |\n| **tmustier/pi-extensions** | One author’s extension collection | Specific QoL tools, especially long-task/session UX |\n\n### Overlap\n\nLazyPi already includes several tmustier extensions:\n\n- `@tmustier/pi-usage-extension`\n- `@tmustier/pi-raw-paste`\n- `@tmustier/pi-ralph-wiggum`\n\nSo LazyPi is partly a superset/curator that pulls from tmustier plus many others.\n\n### Mustier page adds things LazyPi may not include\n\nInteresting extras:\n\n- `/files` — terminal file browser with diffs/line selection\n- `/code` — copy/insert code snippets from agent output\n- `tab-status` — terminal tab indicators for parallel sessions\n- `arcade`, `pi-nes` — fun/nonessential\n\n### Practical recommendation\n\nFor you, I’d treat **Mustier’s extensions as à la carte additions**, not an alternative to LazyPi.\n\n- Use **LazyPi** if setting up a fresh Pi environment.\n- Use **Mustier’s page** to cherry-pick specific extensions you want.\n\nGiven your existing setup, I’d consider:\n\n```bash\npi install npm:@tmustier/pi-files-widget\npi install npm:@tmustier/pi-code-actions\npi install npm:@tmustier/pi-tab-status\n```\n\nI would skip installing the full `git:github.com/tmustier/pi-extensions` bundle unless you want all of it, including arcade/NES stuff.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_02f887b473d5faa9016a085b2cb5a48190a08f734ee6acfa27\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input":836,"output":360,"cacheRead":32768,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":33964,"cost":{"input":0.0041800000000000006,"output":0.0108,"cacheRead":0.016384,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.031364}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1778932523841,"responseId":"resp_02f887b473d5faa9016a085b2c0174819098dea4a450bd245f"}}
{"type":"message","id":"b66ab0f3","parentId":"97cf2069","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:13:11.695Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"what kind of performance hits would we take with installing lazypi"}],"timestamp":1778933591629}}
{"type":"message","id":"468f805b","parentId":"b66ab0f3","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:13:21.008Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Likely **small runtime hit**, bigger **context/tooling/UI clutter hit**.\n\n### Performance costs\n\n- **Startup / extension load:** more extensions = slower Pi startup. Probably noticeable if installing all LazyPi, but not catastrophic.\n- **Prompt/context bloat:** skills, tool descriptions, package instructions can increase system/context size. This is the main real cost: more tokens before every answer, more possible tool choices.\n- **UI rendering overhead:** powerbar, usage tracker, todo/status widgets, themes, etc. add some TUI work. Usually minor.\n- **Background processes:** most extensions don’t run constantly, but things like usage tracking, interactive shell, MCP adapters, memory/tape-style tools may do work when invoked.\n- **Model behavior overhead:** more capabilities can make the agent “think” about more options, sometimes slower/less focused.\n- **Install/update time + disk:** one-time cost; many npm/git packages.\n\n### Highest-cost categories\n\n- **Compound Engineering / many skills**: biggest context/behavior footprint.\n- **MCP / web / memory / subagents**: more tools and richer system prompt.\n- **UI widgets**: small CPU/render overhead, possible visual noise.\n- **Themes**: basically no runtime cost except install size.\n\n### For your setup\n\nYou already have lots of LazyPi-ish pieces installed: subagents, ask-user, web access, interactive shell, memory, autoresearch, Ralph, Compound, themes, etc. Installing all of LazyPi probably gives **duplication more than speed benefits**.\n\nI’d expect:\n- **Pi launch maybe slower**\n- **agent prompt/tool surface more crowded**\n- **little direct CPU hit during normal coding**\n- **token/context overhead more important than machine performance**\n\nRecommendation: run:\n\n```bash\nnpx @robzolkos/lazypi status\n```\n\nThen install only missing useful bits. Avoid “all packages” unless you want a full reset-style distro experience.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_02f887b473d5faa9016a085f5951988190b00217a17662bb02\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input":706,"output":402,"cacheRead":33280,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":34388,"cost":{"input":0.00353,"output":0.01206,"cacheRead":0.01664,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.032229999999999995}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1778933591693,"responseId":"resp_02f887b473d5faa9016a085f5852448190b292543f9c018617"}}
