space checks/tests\n\nNotable custom pieces:\n- `web-tools` — custom web fetch/search extension\n- `pi-mcp` — fork/custom MCP adapter with OAuth + panel UI, mentioned in AGENTS\n- `opencode-cloudflare` — custom provider/auth integration\n- `pi-skill-toggle` — skill discovery/toggle UI\n- `todos`\n- `answer.ts`, `update.ts`, `whimsical.ts`\n\nIts AGENTS says settings use only a few package installs:\n\n```jsonc\n\"packages\": [\n  \"npm:pi-extmgr\",\n  \"npm:@plannotator/pi-extension\",\n  \"npm:pi-interview\"\n]\n```\n\nBut `settings.json` itself wasn’t present in the fetched repo, likely gitignored/private despite being described.\n\n### Comparison to LazyPi\n\nLazyPi is easier:\n```bash\nnpx @robzolkos/lazypi\n```\n\ndmmulroy’s setup is more maintainable if you want to **own the runtime**:\n```bash\nnpm install\nnpm run check\n```\n\nLazyPi gives you many community extensions quickly.  \ndmmulroy gives you fewer, curated/custom pieces with source-controlled behavior.\n\n### My read\n\nFor your `atlas-config`, dmmulroy’s approach is probably the better model:\n\n- keep Pi config in dotfiles\n- avoid giant package surface\n- add only the extensions you actually use\n- own custom glue/extensions when needed\n- version skills/settings/themes explicitly\n\nSo: **LazyPi = bootstrap inspiration/catalog. dmmulroy = architecture pattern worth copying.**","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_02f887b473d5faa9016a085fc4021c8190b774a2da55c2f4b3\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input":517,"output":510,"cacheRead":36864,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":37891,"cost":{"input":0.002585,"output":0.015300000000000001,"cacheRead":0.018432,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.036317}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1778933698541,"responseId":"resp_02f887b473d5faa9016a085fc2c0d4819099cec791bf1de0ff"}}
{"type":"message","id":"a8273456","parentId":"f7b73089","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:59:18.315Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Start autoresearch: find popular pi coding agent projects on github. I'd like a full list of the most popular pi-extensions (more than github stars)\nMy goal is to keep my pi setup (Atlas config) to be very minimal with global installed extensions and skills\n\nalso use a subagent to create github issues for atlas-config repo:\n- create a global slash command that will install pi extensions and skills for different types of project flows: 1. marketing focus (seo, social media, blogging, etc) 2. SSG/SSR website 3. video editing (video creation/editing)\nhave the subagent also create a few options for the flows - i know i want one for consulting on ui/ux and consulting for infra ipsec/pentesting Be careful not to overfit to the benchmarks and do not cheat on the benchmarks."}],"timestamp":1778936358228}}
