{"type":"custom","customType":"web-search-results","data":{"id":"mp8cssk3jcgb1x","type":"fetch","timestamp":1778936396691,"urls":[{"url":"https://github.com/weichenw/pi-subagents","title":"weichenw/pi-subagents","content":"# Repository: weichenw/pi-subagents (fork of nicobailon/pi-subagents)\n[...]\nPi extension for async subagent delegation with truncation, artifacts, and session sharing\n[...]\n- Stars: 0\n- Forks: 0\n- Watchers: 0\n- Open issues: 0\n- Primary language: TypeScript\n- Languages: TypeScript (99.2%), JavaScript (0.8%)\n- Default branch: main\n- Created: 2026-05-13T09:32:58Z\n- Last push: 2026-05-10T19:01:17Z\n- Contributors: 20 (top: nicobailon, marcfargas, claude, SeanThomasWilliams, tmustier, HazAT, akaihola, chenxin-yan, jihunkim0, mikeyobrien)\n[...]\n`pi-subagents` lets Pi delegate work to focused child agents. Use it for code review, scouting, implementation, parallel audits, saved workflows, background jobs, and anything else that benefits from a second or third set of model eyes.\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:pi-subagents\n```\n[...]\n| --- | --- |\n|\n[...]\n, entry points\n[...]\nand where another agent should start. |\n|\n[...]\nresearcher` |\n[...]\nchanges, and a concise research brief. |\n|\n[...]\n` | A concrete\n[...]\nfrom existing context. It should read and\n[...]\n. |\n|\n[...]\nworker` |\n[...]\nwork, including approved\n[...]\nhandoffs. It edits files,\n[...]\napproved decisions instead of\n[...]\n. |\n|\n[...]\nIt checks the\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm\n[...]\nThe `researcher` builtin uses `web_search`, `fetch_content`, and `get_search_content`; those require pi-web-access:\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:pi-web-access\n```\n[...]\n| Field | Notes |\n| --- | --- |\n| `package` | Optional package identifier. A file with `name: scout` and `package: code-analysis` registers as `code-analysis.scout`; serialization keeps `name` and `package` separate. |\n| `tools` | Builtin tool allowlist. `mcp:` entries select direct MCP tools when `pi-mcp-adapter` is installed. |\n| `extensions` | Omitted means normal extensions; empty means no extensions; comma-separated values allowlist specific extensions. |\n| `model` | Default model. Bare ids prefer the current provider when possible, then unique registry matches.\n[...]\n`fallbackModels` | Ordered backup models for provider/model\n[...]\nsuch as quota, auth, timeout, or unavailable model. Ordinary task failures do not trigger fallback.\n[...]\nIf `tools` is omitted, `pi-subagents` does not pass `--tools`, so the child gets Pi’s normal builtin tools. If `tools` is present, regular tool names become an explicit allowlist. `mcp:` entries are split out and forwarded as direct MCP selections. Path-like `tools` entries, such as extension paths or `.ts`/`.js` files, are treated as tool-extension paths rather than builtin tool names.\n[...]\nDirect MCP tools require pi-mcp-adapter. Subagents only receive direct MCP tools when `mcp:` entries are listed in their frontmatter; global `directTools: true` in `mcp.json` is not enough by itself. The generic `mcp` proxy tool can still be used for discovery when available. The adapter caches tool metadata at startup, so after connecting a new MCP server for the first time, restart Pi before relying on direct tools.\n[...]\nBridge activation also requires `pi-intercom` to be installed and enabled through `pi install npm:pi-intercom` or a legacy\n[...]\n, a targetable current\n[...]\n`pi-intercom` in any explicit agent `extensions` allowlist.","error":null},{"url":"https://registry.npmjs.org/@ifi/pi-extension-subagents","title":"@ifi/pi-extension-subagents","content":"Full-featured subagent orchestration extension for pi, built on top of nicobailon/pi-subagents.\n[...]\nnpm i @ifi/\n[...]\n-subagents\n[...]\n- Repository: https://github.com/ifiokjr/oh-pi (packages/subagents)\n- Homepage: https://github.com/ifiokjr/oh-pi/tree/main/packages/subagents\n- Weekly Downloads: 307\n- License: MIT\n- Unpacked Size: 1.7MB\n- Total Files: 57\n- Keywords: chains, delegation, multi-agent, pi, pi-coding-agent, pi-package, subagents\n- 1 Dependencies\n- 2 Dependents\n- 16 Versions\n[...]\nPi extension for delegating tasks to subagents with chains, parallel execution, TUI clarification, and async support.\n[...]\nBuilt on top of nicobailon/pi-subagents (MIT).\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:@ifi/pi-extension-subagents\n```\n[...]\nOr install it as part of the full oh-pi bundle:\n[...]\n```bash\nnpx @ifi/oh-pi\n```\n[...]\nOr use the package installer directly:\n[...]\n```bash\nnpx @ifi/pi-extension-subagents\nnpx @ifi/pi-extension-subagents --local\n```\n[...]\n> **Note:** The `researcher` agent uses `web_search`, `fetch_content`, and `get_search_content` tools which require the pi-web-access extension. Install it with `pi install npm:pi-web-access`.\n> \n> **Note:** `artist` and `multimodal-summariser` work best when the runtime can access the relevant media files directly. `artist` prioritizes production-ready SVG and concrete image briefs when raster generation is not available as a tool.\n[...]\n```yaml\n---\nname: scout\ndescription: Fast codebase recon\ntools: read, grep, find, ls, bash, mcp:chrome-devtools # mcp: requires pi-mcp-adapter\nextensions: # absent=all, empty=none, csv=allowlist\ncategory: quick-discovery # optional delegated routing category\nthinking: high # off, minimal, low, medium, high, xhigh\nskill: safe-bash, chrome-devtools # comma-separated skills to inject\n[...]\n: context.md #\n[...]\ncontext.md\ndefaultReads: context.md #\n[...]\n-separated files\n[...]\nread\ndefaultProgress: true # maintain progress.md\ninteractive: true # (parsed but not enforced in v1\n[...]\n**MCP Tools**\n\nAgents can use MCP server tools directly (requires the pi-mcp-adapter extension). Add `mcp:` prefixed entries to the `tools` field:\n[...]\n```yaml\n# All tools from a server\ntools: read, bash, mcp:chrome-devtools\n[...]\n# Specific tools from a server\ntools: read, bash, mcp:github/search_repositories, mcp:github/get_file_contents\n```\n[...]\nThe `mcp:` items are additive — they don't affect which builtins the agent gets. `tools: mcp:chrome-devtools` (with no regular tools listed) gives the agent all default builtins plus chrome-devtools tools. To restrict builtins, list them explicitly: `tools: read, bash, mcp:chrome-devtools`.\n[...]\nSubagents only get direct MCP tools when `mcp:` items are explicitly listed. Even if your `mcp.json` has `directTools: true` globally, a subagent without `mcp:` in its frontmatter won't get any direct tools — keeping it lean. The `mcp` proxy tool is still available for discovery if needed.\n[...]\nThe MCP adapter's metadata cache must be populated for direct tools to work. On the first session with a new MCP server, tools will only be available through the `mcp` proxy. Restart Pi after the first session and direct tools become available.\n[...]\n`, `extensions` controls extension sandboxing, and `tools\n[...]\nsupports `mcp:`\n[...]\ndirect MCP tools.\n[...]\n| Package | Version |\n| --- | --- |\n| @mariozechner/pi-agent-core | >=0.56.1 |\n| @mariozechner/pi-ai | >=0.56.1 |\n| @mariozechner/pi-coding-agent | >=0.56.1 |\n| @mariozechner/pi-tui | >=0.56.1 |\n| @sinclair/typebox | * |","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/ghul0/pi-mcp-adapter","title":"ghul0/pi-mcp-adapter","content":"# Repository: ghul0/pi-mcp-adapter (fork of nicobailon/pi-mcp-adapter)\n[...]\nToken-efficient MCP adapter for Pi coding agent\n[...]\n- Stars: 0\n- Forks: 0\n- Watchers: 0\n- Open issues: 0\n- Primary language: TypeScript\n- Languages: TypeScript (99.0%), JavaScript (1.0%)\n- License: MIT License (MIT)\n- Default branch: main\n- Created: 2026-03-19T13:26:23Z\n- Last push: 2026-05-08T05:37:24Z\n- Contributors: 4 (top: nicobailon, monotykamary, limas-yalo, HazAT)\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:pi-mcp-adapter\n```\n[...]\nIf you prefer the terminal, you can also run `pi-mcp-adapter init` after install to scan for host-specific configs and add missing compatibility imports to the Pi agent dir (`~/.pi/agent/mcp.json` by default, or `$PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR/mcp.json` when set).\n[...]\n**Subagent integration:** If you use the subagent extension, agents can request direct MCP tools in their frontmatter with `mcp:server-name` syntax. See the subagent README for details.\n[...]\n**Native rendering:** On macOS, if Glimpse is installed (`pi install npm:glimpseui`), UIs\n[...]\nin a native WKWebView window instead of a browser tab. Set `MCP_UI_VIEWER=browser` to force the browser, or `MCP_UI_VIEWER=glimpse` to require native rendering\n[...]\n-mcp-\n[...]\nPrefer `.mcp.json` for project-local shared MCP config. Use `.pi/mcp.json` only when you need a Pi-specific project override. Project files override both user-global shared MCP config and Pi global overrides.\n[...]\n| Command | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `/mcp` | Interactive panel and first-run onboarding surface |\n| `/mcp setup` | Guided setup for imports, a minimal `.mcp.json`, RepoPrompt quick-add, and config-path inspection |\n| `/mcp tools` | List all tools |\n| `/mcp reconnect` | Reconnect all servers |\n| `/mcp reconnect ` | Connect or reconnect a single server |\n| `/mcp-auth ` | OAuth setup |","error":null},{"url":"https://pi.dev/packages","title":"Package Catalog - Pi Coding Agent","content":"claude-code-tmuxDurable tmux controller for interactive Claude Code sessions, with a Pi extension and skill.2m ago pi-git-commandsPi agent extension: /git-commit and /git-push with LLM-generated Conventional Commits messages6m ago my-pi-themes14 custom themes for Pi Coding Agent: neapple, monokai-pro, onedark, e-ink, tokyo-dark, and more6m ago pi-cyber-uiStandalone Pi UI package with a cyber-inspired theme, custom editor, footer, and lightweight working indicator.14m ago @narumitw/pi-subagentsPi extension for delegating work to specialized isolated subagents.19m ago @narumitw/pi-statuslinePi extension that replaces the footer with an information-rich statusline.19m ago @narumitw/pi-retryPublic pi extension that retries empty-detail provider unknown errors.19m ago @narumitw/pi-python-lspPi extension that exposes ty and Ruff language-server tools for Python.19m ago\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:\n[...]\n-mode` Copy\n[...]\n### pi-subagents\n[...]\nPi extension for delegating tasks to subagents with chains, parallel execution, and TUI clarification\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-subagents` Copy\n[...]\n### pi-mcp-adapter\n[...]\nMCP (Model Context Protocol) adapter extension for Pi coding agent\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-mcp-adapter` Copy\n[...]\n### pi-web-access\n[...]\nWeb search, URL fetching, GitHub repo cloning, PDF extraction, YouTube video understanding, and local video analysis for Pi coding agent\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-web-access` Copy\n[...]\n### @tintinweb/pi-subagents\n[...]\nagents to pi.\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@tintinweb/pi-subagents` Copy","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-mcp-adapter","title":"Token-efficient MCP adapter for Pi coding agent - GitHub","content":"# Repository: nicobailon\n[...]\n-mcp-adapter\n[...]\nToken-efficient MCP adapter for Pi coding agent\n[...]\n- Stars: 687\n- Forks: 122\n- Watchers: 3\n- Open issues: 56\n- Primary language: TypeScript\n- Languages: TypeScript (99.1%), JavaScript (0.9%)\n- License: MIT License (MIT)\n- Topics: ai, claude, coding-agent, extension, llm, mcp, model-context-protocol, pi\n- Default branch: main\n- Created: 2026-01-19T23:23:58Z\n- Last push: 2026-05-13T16:23:35Z\n- Contributors: 5 (top: nicobailon, monotykamary, limas-yalo, HazAT, mattzcarey)\n- Releases: 31\n- Latest release: v2.6.1 (2026-05-13T16:24:14Z)\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:pi-mcp-adapter\n```\n[...]\nIf you prefer the terminal, you can also run `pi-mcp-adapter init` after install to scan for host-specific configs and add missing compatibility imports to the Pi agent dir (`~/.pi/agent/mcp.json` by default, or `$PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR/mcp.json` when set).\n[...]\n**Subagent integration:** If you use the subagent extension, agents can request direct MCP tools in their frontmatter with `mcp:server-name` syntax. See the subagent README for details.\n[...]\n**Native rendering:** On macOS, if Glimpse is installed (`pi install npm:glimpseui`), UIs\n[...]\nin a native WKWebView window instead of a browser tab. Set `MCP_UI_VIEWER=browser` to force the browser, or `MCP_UI_VIEWER=glimpse` to require native rendering.\n[...]\n```bash\nnpm install\n[...]\nnpm run build\nnpm run install-local\n[...]\n`pi-mcp-adapter init\n[...]\ndetects these host-specific\n[...]\nand adds missing imports\n[...]\ndir config for you.\n[...]\nPrefer `.mcp.json` for project-\n[...]\nshared MCP config. Use `.pi/mcp.json` only when you need a Pi-specific project override. Project files override both user-global shared MCP config and Pi global overrides.\n[...]\n| Command | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `/mcp` | Interactive panel and first-run onboarding surface |\n| `/mcp setup` | Guided setup for imports, a minimal `.mcp.json`, RepoPrompt quick-add, and config-path inspection |\n| `/mcp tools` | List all tools |\n| `/mcp reconnect` | Reconnect all servers |\n| `/mcp reconnect ` | Connect or reconnect a single server |\n| `/mcp logout ` | Clear stored OAuth credentials for a server and disconnect it |\n| `/mcp-auth` | Open an OAuth server picker in interactive UI sessions |\n| `/mcp-auth ` | OAuth setup for a specific server |","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/tintinweb/pi-subagents","title":"GitHub - tintinweb/pi-subagents: Sub-agents for pi with Claude Code ...","content":"Sub-agents for pi with Claude Code look and feel — parallel execution, live widget, custom agent types, mid-run steering and more ...\n[...]\n- Stars: 309\n- Forks: 65\n- Watchers: 0\n- Open issues: 20\n- Primary language: TypeScript\n- Languages: TypeScript\n- License: MIT License (MIT)\n- Default branch: master\n- Homepage: https://www.npmjs.com/package/@tintinweb/pi-subagents\n- Created: 2026-03-05T10:08:27Z\n- Last push: 2026-05-14T17:20:04Z\n- Contributors: 20 (top: tintinweb, rylwin, screenfluent, bradwright, gotgenes, Zeng-Zer, Nachompiras, any-victor, Copilot, edxeth)\n- Releases: 29\n- Latest release: v0.7.3 (2026-05-14T14:20:01Z)\n[...]\nA pi extension that brings **Claude Code-style autonomous sub-agents** to pi. Spawn specialized agents that run in isolated sessions — each with its own tools, system prompt, model, and thinking level. Run them in foreground or background, steer them mid-run, resume completed sessions, and define your own custom agent types.\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:@tintinweb/pi-subagents\n```\n[...]\nOr load directly for development:\n[...]\n-e ./\n[...]\n/index.ts\n[...]\n| Field | Default | Description |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `description` | filename | Agent description shown in tool listings |\n| `display_name` | — | Display name for UI (e.g. widget, agent list) |\n| `tools` | all 7 | Comma-separated built-in tools: read, bash, edit, write, grep, find, ls. `none` for no tools |\n| `extensions` | `true` | Inherit MCP/extension tools. `false` to disable |\n| `skills` | `true` | Inherit skills from parent. Can be a comma-separated list of skill names to preload (see Skill Preloading for discovery locations) |\n| `memory` | — | Persistent agent memory scope: `project`, `local`, or `user`. Auto-detects read-only agents |\n| `disallowed_tools` | — | Comma-separated tools to deny even if extensions provide them |\n| `isolation` | — |\n[...]\nnet\"`) |\n[...]\n| inherit | off\n[...]\nor omit for\n[...]\n| `inherit_context` | `false` | Fork parent conversation into agent |\n| `run_in_background` | `false` | Run in background by\n[...]\n|\n| `isolated` | `false` | No extension/MCP tools, only built-in |\n| `enabled` | `true` | Set to `false` to disable an agent (useful for hiding a default agent per-project) |\n[...]\n| Parameter | Type | Required | Description |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `prompt` | string | yes | The task for the agent |\n| `description` | string | yes | Short 3-5 word summary (shown in UI) |\n| `subagent_type` | string | yes | Agent type (built-in or custom) |\n| `model` | string | no | Model — `provider/modelId` or fuzzy name (`\"haiku\"`, `\"sonnet\"`) |\n| `thinking` | string | no | Thinking level: off, minimal, low, medium, high, xhigh |\n| `max_turns` | number | no | Max agentic turns. Omit for unlimited (default) |\n| `run_in_background` | boolean | no | Run without blocking |\n| `resume` | string | no | Agent ID to resume a previous session |\n| `isolated` | boolean | no | No extension/MCP tools |\n| `isolation` | `\"worktree\"` | no | Run in an isolated git worktree |\n| `inherit_context` | boolean | no | Fork parent conversation into agent |","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/espennilsen/pi/tree/main/extensions/pi-subagent","title":"pi/extensions/pi-subagent at main · espennilsen/pi · GitHub","content":"pi/extensions/pi-subagent at main · espennilsen/pi · GitHub\n[...]\nStar 71\n[...]\n# pi-subagent\n[...]\n# @e9n/pi-subagent\n[...]\nParallel task delegation extension for pi. Spawn isolated pi subprocesses for single, parallel, chain, orchestrator, and pool-based agent workflows.\n[...]\n- Single — delegate one task to a named agent subprocess\n- Parallel — run multiple independent tasks concurrently; results stream back as they complete\n- Chain — pipeline tasks where each step receives the previous output via`{previous}`\n- Orchestrator — hierarchical agent trees where agents spawn and message each other autonomously\n- Pool — long-lived agents with persistent context; send follow-up messages without losing state\n- Agent discovery — reads agent definitions from`~/.pi/agent/agents/*.md` and`.pi/agents/*.md`\n- Extension isolation — subagents run with`--no-extensions` by default; whitelist only what's needed\n- System prompt injection — injects available agents and usage patterns into the LLM context automatically\n- Bundled skill — includes skill definitions for agent orchestration patterns\n\n## Setup\n[...]\nAdd to`~/.pi/agent/settings.json` or`.pi/settings.json`:\n\n```\n{\n  \"pi-subagent\": {\n    \"maxConcurrent\": 4,\n    \"maxTotal\": 8,\n    \"timeoutMs\": 600000,\n    \"model\": null,\n    \"extensions\": [],\n    \"blockedExtensions\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n[...]\n| Key | Default | Description |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `maxConcurrent` | `4` | Max subagents running in parallel |\n| `maxTotal` | `8` | Max total subagents per session |\n| `timeoutMs` | `600000` | Subprocess timeout in ms (10 min) |\n| `model` | `null` | Model override for all subagents (`null`= use default) |\n| `extensions` | `[]` | Extension paths whitelisted for all subagents |\n| `blockedExtensions` | `[see below]` | Extensions subagents can never load. Default:`pi-webserver`,`pi-cron`,`pi-heartbeat`,`pi-channels`,`pi-web-dashboard`,`pi-telemetry`.`pi-subagent` is always blocked. |\n[...]\n| Parameter | Type | Description |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `agent` | string | Agent name (from discovered`.\n[...]\n` definitions) |\n| `task` | string | Task prompt for the subagent |\n| `model` | string | Model override for this invocation |\n| `thinking` | string | Thinking budget:`off`,`minimal`,`low`,`medium`,`high`,`xhigh` |\n| `extensions` | string[] | Extra extension paths to whitelist for this invocation |\n| `noTools` | boolean | Run without\n[...]\ntools |\n| `agentScope` | string | Agent discovery scope:`user`,`project`, or`both`\n[...]\n```\npi install npm:@e9n/pi-subagent\n```","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/paperwave/pi-mcp-adapter","title":"paperwave/pi-mcp-adapter","content":"# Repository: paperwave/pi-mcp-adapter (fork of nicobailon/pi-mcp-adapter)\n[...]\nToken-efficient MCP adapter for Pi coding agent\n[...]\n- Stars: 0\n- Forks: 0\n- Watchers: 0\n- Open issues: 0\n- Primary language: TypeScript\n- Languages: TypeScript (99.3%), JavaScript (0.7%)\n- License: MIT License (MIT)\n- Default branch: main\n- Created: 2026-04-16T09:16:11Z\n- Last push: 2026-04-13T18:24:57Z\n- Contributors: 3 (top: nicobailon, monotykamary, HazAT)\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:pi-mcp-adapter\n```\n[...]\n**Subagent integration:** If you use the subagent extension, agents can request direct MCP tools in their frontmatter with `mcp:server-name` syntax. See the subagent README for details.\n[...]\nSearch includes both MCP tools and Pi tools (from extensions). Pi tools appear first with `[pi tool]` prefix. Space-separated words are OR'd.\n[...]\n|\n| ---\n[...]\n--- |\n| `/\n[...]\nstatus, tool toggles, reconnect) |\n| `/mcp tools` | List\n[...]\ntools |\n| `/mcp reconnect\n[...]\n|\n|\n[...]\nmcp reconnect","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/cmf/pi-subagent","title":"cmf/pi-subagent","content":"# Repository: cmf/pi-subagent\n[...]\n- Stars: 0\n- Forks: 0\n- Watchers: 0\n- Open issues: 0\n- Primary language: TypeScript\n- Languages: TypeScript\n- Default branch: main\n- Created: 2026-01-08T23:47:28Z\n- Last push: 2026-01-08T23:47:32Z\n- Contributors: 1 (top: cmf)\n[...]\n# pi-subagent\n[...]\nSubagent infrastructure for pi coding agent extensions. Spawn and orchestrate pi subprocesses with full observability.\n[...]\n```bash\nnpm install @cmf/pi-subagent\n```\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport type { ExtensionAPI } from \"@mariozechner/pi-coding-agent\";\nimport { invokeAgentWithUI, registerSubagentRenderer } from \"@cmf/pi-subagent\";\n[...]\n## Peer Dependencies\n[...]\nRequires pi coding agent >= 0.38.0:\n[...]\n```json\n{\n  \"peerDependencies\": {\n    \"@mariozechner/pi-ai\": \">=0.38.0\",\n    \"@mariozechner/pi-coding-agent\": \">=0.38.0\",\n    \"@mariozechner/pi-tui\": \">=0.38.0\"\n  }\n}\n```","error":null},{"url":"https://libraries.io/npm/@jordyvd%2Fpi-mcp-adapter","title":"@jordyvd/pi-mcp-adapter 2.3.0 on npm - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software","content":"@jordyvd/pi-mcp-adapter 2.3.0 on npm - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software\n[...]\n# @jordyvd/pi-mcp-adapter Release 2.3.0\n[...]\nMCP (Model Context Protocol) adapter extension for Pi coding agent\n[...]\n# Pi MCP Adapter\n[...]\nUse MCP servers with Pi without burning your context window.\n[...]\n```\npi install npm:pi-mcp-adapter\n```\n[...]\nafter installation.\n[...]\nSubagent integration: If you use the subagent extension, agents can request direct MCP tools in their frontmatter with`mcp:server-name` syntax. See the subagent README for details.\n[...]\n| Command | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `/mcp` | Interactive panel (server status, tool toggles, reconnect/auth via`Ctrl+R`, auth notices) |\n| `/mcp tools` | List all tools |\n| `/mcp reconnect` | Reconnect all servers |\n| `/mcp reconnect ` | Connect or reconnect a single server |\n| `/mcp auth` | Show OAuth status, flow, registration strategy, storage hints, and browser/no-browser behavior |\n| `/mcp auth ` | Intentionally start or retry auth for one OAuth-configured HTTP server (`authorization_code` browser flow or`client_credentials` token exchange) |\n| `/mcp-auth` | Compatibility launcher for`/mcp auth` |\n| `/mcp-auth ` | Compatibility launcher for`/mcp auth ` |","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/sdk.md","title":"packages/coding-agent/docs/sdk.md at main · earendil-works/pi","content":"```typescript\nimport { AuthStorage, createAgentSession, ModelRegistry, SessionManager } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n[...]\n```bash\nnpm install @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\n```\n[...]\nThe SDK is included in the main package. No separate installation needed.\n[...]\n`createAgentSession()` uses a `ResourceLoader` to supply extensions, skills, prompt templates, themes, and context files. If you do not provide one, it uses `DefaultResourceLoader` with standard discovery.\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport { createAgentSession } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n[...]\nExtensions are loaded by the `ResourceLoader`. `DefaultResourceLoader` discovers extensions from `~/.pi/agent/extensions/`, `.pi/extensions/`, and settings.json extension sources.\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport { createAgentSession, DefaultResourceLoader } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n[...]\nconst loader = new DefaultResourceLoader({\n  additionalExtensionPaths: [\"/path/to/my-extension.ts\"],\n  extensionFactories: [\n    (pi) => {\n      pi.on(\"agent_start\", () => {\n        console.log(\"[Inline Extension] Agent starting\");\n      });\n    },\n  ],\n});\n[...]\nawait loader.reload();\n\nconst { session } = await createAgentSession({ resourceLoader: loader });\n[...]\nExtensions can register tools, subscribe to events, add commands, and more. See extensions.md for the full API.\n[...]\n**Event Bus:** Extensions can communicate via `pi.events`. Pass a shared `eventBus` to `DefaultResourceLoader` if you need to emit or listen from outside:\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport { create\n[...]\nBus, DefaultResourceLoader } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n[...]\nfrom \"@e\n[...]\n## ResourceLoader\n[...]\nUse `DefaultResourceLoader` to discover extensions, skills, prompts, themes, and context files.\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport {\n  DefaultResourceLoader\n[...]\n} from \"@e\n[...]\nFor extension types, see extensions.md for the full API.","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md","title":"pi/packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md at main - GitHub","content":"```md\n> pi can create extensions. Ask it to build one for your use case.\n\n# Extensions\n[...]\nExtensions are TypeScript modules that extend pi's behavior. They can subscribe to lifecycle events, register custom tools callable by the LLM, add commands, and more.\n[...]\n> **Placement for /reload:** Put extensions in `~/.pi/agent/extensions/` (global) or `.pi/extensions/` (project-local) for auto-discovery. Use `pi -e ./path.ts` only for quick tests. Extensions in auto-discovered locations can be hot-reloaded with `/reload`.\n[...]\n.ts`:\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport type { ExtensionAPI } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\nimport { Type } from \"typebox\";\n[...]\nworld\"}!`,\n[...]\n},\n[...]\nTest with `--extension` (or `-e`) flag:\n[...]\n## Extension Locations\n[...]\n> **Security:** Extensions run with your full system permissions and can execute arbitrary code. Only install from sources you trust.\n[...]\nExtensions are auto-discovered from:\n[...]\n| Location | Scope |\n|----------|-------|\n| `~/.pi/agent/extensions/*.ts` | Global (all projects) |\n| `~/.pi/agent/extensions/*/index.ts` | Global (subdirectory) |\n| `.pi/extensions/*.ts` | Project-local |\n| `.pi/extensions/*/index.ts` | Project-local (subdirectory) |\n[...]\n## Available Imports\n[...]\n| Package | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` | Extension types (`ExtensionAPI`, `ExtensionContext`, events) |\n| `typebox` | Schema definitions for tool parameters |\n| `@earendil-works/pi-ai` | AI utilities (`StringEnum` for Google-compatible enums) |\n| `@earendil-works/pi-tui` | TUI components for custom rendering |\n[...]\nnpm dependencies work too. Add a `package.json` next to your extension (or in a parent directory), run `npm install`, and imports from `node_modules/` are resolved automatically.\n[...]\nFor distributed pi packages installed with `pi install` (npm or git), runtime deps must be in `dependencies`. Package installation uses production installs (`npm install --omit=dev`) by default, so `devDependencies` are not available at runtime; when `npmCommand` is configured, git packages use plain `install` for compatibility with wrappers.\n[...]\nWriting an Extension\n[...]\nAn extension exports a default factory function that receives `ExtensionAPI`. The factory can be synchronous or asynchronous:\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport type { ExtensionAPI } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n[...]\nexport default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {\n  // Subscribe to events\n  pi.on(\"event_name\", async (event, ctx) => {\n    // ctx.ui for user interaction\n    const ok = await ctx.ui.confirm(\"Title\", \"Are you sure?\");\n    ctx.ui.notify(\"Done!\", \"success\");\n    ctx.ui.setStatus(\"my-ext\", \"Processing...\");  // Footer status\n    ctx.ui.setWidget(\"my-ext\", [\"Line 1\", \"Line 2\"]);  // Widget above editor (default)\n  });\n\n  // Register tools, commands, shortcuts, flags\n  pi.registerTool({ ... });\n  pi.registerCommand(\"name\", { ... });\n  pi.registerShortcut(\"ctrl+x\", { ... });\n  pi.registerFlag(\"my-flag\", { ... });\n}\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport type { ExtensionAPI } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n[...]\n**Package with dependencies** - for extensions that need npm packages:\n[...]\nextension\",\n[...]\n.0\",\n[...]\n\"chalk\": \"^5.0.0\"\n  },\n  \"pi\": {\n    \"extensions\": [\"./src/index.\n[...]\n\"]\n  }\n}\n[...]\nRun `npm install` in the extension directory, then imports from `node_modules/` work automatically.\n[...]\nimport type { ExtensionAPI } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n[...]\n### pi.registerTool\n[...]\n### pi.registerCommand(name, options)\n[...]\n### pi.registerProvider(name, config)","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/custom-provider.md","title":"packages/coding-agent/docs/custom-provider.md at main · earendil-works/pi","content":"Extensions can register custom model providers via `pi.registerProvider()`. This enables:\n[...]\nrequests through corporate proxies or API gateways\n- **Custom\n[...]\nself-hosted\n[...]\n- **OAuth\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport type { ExtensionAPI } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n[...]\nexport default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {\n  // Override baseUrl for existing provider\n  pi.registerProvider(\"anthropic\", {\n    baseUrl: \"https://proxy.example.com\"\n  });\n\n  // Register new provider with models\n  pi.registerProvider(\"my-provider\", {\n    name: \"My Provider\",\n    baseUrl: \"https://api.example.com\",\n    apiKey: \"MY_API_KEY\",\n    api: \"openai-completions\",\n    models: [\n      {\n        id: \"my-model\",\n        name: \"My Model\",\n        reasoning: false,\n        input: [\"text\", \"image\"],\n        cost: { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0 },\n        contextWindow: 128000,\n        maxTokens: 4096\n      }\n    ]\n  });\n}\n[...]\nThe extension factory can also be `async`. For dynamic model discovery, fetch and register models in the factory instead of `session_start`. pi waits for the factory before startup continues, so the provider is available during interactive startup and to `pi --list-models`.\n[...]\n## Register New Provider\n[...]\nTo add a completely new provider, specify `models` along with the required configuration.\n[...]\nIf the model list comes from a remote endpoint, use an async extension factory:\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport type { ExtensionAPI } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n[...]\nexport default async function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {\n  const response = await fetch(\"http://localhost:1234/v1/models\");\n  const payload = (await response.json()) as {\n    data: Array<{\n      id: string;\n      name?: string;\n      context_window?: number;\n      max_tokens?: number;\n    }>;\n  };\n\n  pi.registerProvider(\"local-openai\", {\n    baseUrl: \"http://localhost:1234/v1\",\n    apiKey: \"LOCAL_OPENAI_API_KEY\",\n    api: \"openai-completions\",\n    models: payload.data.map((model) => ({\n      id: model.id,\n      name: model.name ?? model.id,\n      reasoning: false,\n      input: [\"text\"],\n      cost: { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0 },\n      contextWindow: model.context_window ?? 128000,\n      maxTokens: model.max_tokens ?? 4096,\n    })),\n  });\n}\n[...]\nThis registers the fetched models before startup finishes.\n[...]\n.registerProvider\n[...]\nmy-llm\n[...]\nAPI_KEY\n[...]\napi:\n[...]\nopenai-completions\n[...]\nmy-llm\n[...]\n: \"My LLM Large\n[...]\nreason\n[...]\n// supports extended\n[...]\nmaxTokens:\n[...]\n6384\n[...]\nWhen `models` is provided, it **replaces** all existing models for that provider.\n[...]\n```typescript\ninterface ProviderConfig {\n  /** Display name for the provider in UI such as /login. */\n  name?: string;\n\n  /** API endpoint URL. Required when defining models. */\n  baseUrl?: string;\n\n  /** API key or environment variable name. Required when defining models (unless oauth). */\n  apiKey?: string;\n\n  /** API type for streaming. Required at provider or model level when defining models. */\n  api?: Api;\n\n  /** Custom streaming implementation for non-standard APIs. */\n  streamSimple?: (\n    model: Model<Api>,\n    context: Context,\n    options?: SimpleStreamOptions\n  ) => AssistantMessageEventStream;\n\n  /** Custom headers to include in requests. Values can be env var names. */\n  headers?: Record<string, string>;\n\n  /** If true, adds Authorization: Bearer header with the resolved API key. */\n  authHeader?: boolean;\n\n  /** Models to register. If provided, replaces all existing models for this provider. */\n  models?: ProviderModelConfig[];\n\n  /** OAuth provider for /login support. */\n  oauth?: {\n    name: string;\n    login(callbacks: OAuthLoginCallbacks): Promise<OAuthCredentials>;\n    refreshToken(credentials: OAuthCredentials): Promise<OAuthCredentials>;\n    getApiKey(credentials: OAuthCredentials): string;\n    modifyModels?(models: Model<Api>[], credentials: OAuthCredentials): Model<Api>[];\n  };\n}","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/quickstart.md","title":"pi/packages/coding-agent/docs/quickstart.md at main - GitHub","content":"# File: earendil-works/pi/packages/coding-agent/docs/quickstart.md\n\n- Repository: earendil-works/pi | AI agent toolkit: coding agent CLI, unified LLM API, TUI & web UI libraries, Slack bot, vLLM pods | 49K stars | TypeScript\n- Branch: main\n\n```md\n# Quickstart\n\nThis page gets you from install to a useful first pi session.\n\n## Install\n\nPi is distributed as an npm package:\n\n```bash\nnpm install -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\n```\n\nThen start pi in the project directory you want it to work on:\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/project\npi\n```\n\n## Authenticate\n\nPi can use subscription providers through `/login`, or API-key providers through environment variables or the auth file.\n\n### Option 1: subscription login\n\nStart pi and run:\n\n```text\n/login\n```\n\nThen select a provider. Built-in subscription logins include Claude Pro/Max, ChatGPT Plus/Pro (Codex), and GitHub Copilot.\n\n### Option 2: API key\n\nSet an API key before launching pi:\n\n```bash\nexport ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...\npi\n```\n\nYou can also run `/login` and select an API-key provider to store the key in `~/.pi/agent/auth.json`.\n\nSee Providers for all supported providers, environment variables, and cloud-provider setup.\n\n## First session\n\nOnce pi starts, type a request and press Enter:\n\n```text\nSummarize this repository and tell me how to run its checks.\n```\n\nBy default, pi gives the model four tools:\n\n- `read` - read files\n- `write` - create or overwrite files\n- `edit` - patch files\n- `bash` - run shell commands\n\nAdditional built-in read-only tools (`grep`, `find`, `ls`) are available through tool options. Pi runs in your current working directory and can modify files there. Use git or another checkpointing workflow if you want easy rollback.\n\n## Give pi project instructions\n\nPi loads context files at startup. Add an `AGENTS.md` file to tell it how to work in a project:\n\n```markdown\n# Project Instructions\n\n- Run `npm run check` after code changes.\n- Do not run production migrations locally.\n- Keep responses concise.\n```\n\nPi loads:\n\n- `~/.pi/agent/AGENTS.md` for global instructions\n- `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md` from parent directories and the current directory\n\nRestart pi, or run `/reload`, after changing context files.\n\n## Common things to try\n\n### Reference files\n\nType `@` in the editor to fuzzy-search files, or pass files on the command line:\n\n```bash\npi @README.md \"Summarize this\"\npi @src/app.ts @src/app.test.ts \"Review these together\"\n```\n\nImages can be pasted with Ctrl+V (Alt+V on Windows) or dragged into supported terminals.\n\n### Run shell commands\n\nIn interactive mode:\n\n```text\n!npm run lint\n```\n\nThe command output is sent to the model. Use `!!command` to run a command without adding its output to the model context.\n\n### Switch models\n\nUse `/model` or Ctrl+L to choose a model. Use Shift+Tab to cycle thinking level. Use Ctrl+P / Shift+Ctrl+P to cycle through scoped models.\n\n### Continue later\n\nSessions are saved automatically:\n\n```bash\npi -c                  # Continue most recent session\npi -r                  # Browse previous sessions\npi --session <path|id> # Open a specific session\n```\n\nInside pi, use `/resume`, `/new`, `/tree`, `/fork`, and `/clone` to manage sessions.\n\n### Non-interactive mode\n\nFor one-shot prompts:\n\n```bash\npi -p \"Summarize this codebase\"\ncat README.md | pi -p \"Summarize this text\"\npi -p @screenshot.png \"What's in this image?\"\n```\n\nUse `--mode json` for JSON event output or `--mode rpc` for process integration.\n\n## Next steps\n\n- Using Pi - interactive mode, slash commands, sessions, context files, and CLI reference.\n- Providers - authentication and model setup.\n- Settings - global and project configuration.\n- Keybindings - shortcuts and customization.\n- Pi Packages - install shared extensions, skills, prompts, and themes.\n\nPlatform notes: Windows, Termux, tmux, Terminal setup, Shell aliases.\n\n```","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/releases/tag/v0.73.1","title":"v0.73.1","content":"- **Self-update support for the npm scope migration**: `pi update --self` now supports the upcoming package rename from `@mariozechner/pi-coding-agent` to `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`. After the new package is published, existing global installs can update through the normal self-update flow; pi will uninstall the old global package and install the package name returned by the version check endpoint.\n[...]\n- Changed `pi update --self` to honor the active package name returned by the Pi version check endpoint, defaulting to the current package when omitted and uninstalling the old global package before installing a renamed package.\n- Changed extension loading to use upstream `jiti` 2.7 instead of the `@mariozechner/jiti` fork (#4244 by @pi0).\n- Changed `models.json` parsing to allow comments and trailing commas (#4162 by @julien-c).","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/README.md","title":"README.md at main · earendil-works/pi","content":"* **@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent**: Interactive coding agent CLI\n* **@earendil-works/pi-agent-core**: Agent runtime with tool calling and state management\n* **@earendil-works/pi-ai**: Unified multi-provider LLM API (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, …)\n[...]\n| Package | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **@earendil-works/pi-ai** | Unified multi-provider LLM API (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, etc.) |\n| **@earendil-works/pi-agent-core** | Agent runtime with tool calling and state management |\n| **@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent** | Interactive coding agent CLI |\n| **@earendil-works/pi-tui** | Terminal UI library with differential rendering |\n| **@earendil-works/pi-web-ui** | Web components for AI chat interfaces |","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/issues/645","title":"Extension package management and hot reload · Issue #645 · earendil-works/pi","content":"--- | --- | --- | ---\n[...]\nnpm | `npm:@foo/bar@1.0.0\n[...]\nnode_modules\n[...]\n| ` /.pi/npm\n[...]\ngithub.com/user/repo@v1`\n[...]\n`~/.pi/agent/git/ / /` | ` /.pi/git/ / /` |\n[...]\n```bash\n# Global (default)\npi install npm:@foo/bar@1.0.0      # npm install -g @foo/bar@1.0.0\npi install git:github.com/u/r@v1   # clone to ~/.pi/agent/git/github.com/u/r/\npi install ./my-extension/         # add to settings.json, npm install if package.json exists\n[...]\n# Local (-l flag)\npi install -l npm:@foo/bar@1.0.0   # add to .pi/npm/package.json, npm install\npi install -l git:github.com/u/r@v1\n[...]\npi-extension-pack\",\n[...]\nextensions\": [\"./src/index\n[...]\n/other.\n[...]\n\"],\n    \"\n[...]\n\"],\n[...]\n\"],\n[...]\n\"]\n  }\n}\n[...]\nPackageManager reads the `pi` field and returns those paths. If no `pi` field, returns the directory for default discovery rules.\n[...]\nvia `pi.registerFlag()`. This requires loading extensions BEFORE full CLI parsing.\n[...]\nnode_modules\n[...]\nPinning: npm with @version pinned. git with @tag\n[...]\n@commit pinned\n[...]\nPinned sources do not update\n[...]\nNo pin means update pulls latest.\n[...]\n> - npm: and git: are only valid for extensions. Packages can provide extensions, skills, prompts, themes via pi field or auto discovery when no pi field exists.\n> - CLI npm or git extension sources install into /tmp/pi-extensions/ for that run only. No settings changes.\n[...]\n> - PackageManager is invoked by DefaultResourceLoader on reload. It installs missing sources automatically. Progress callbacks are needed so TUI can show status.\n[...]\nFull implementation plan for #\n[...]\n> Scope and intent\n[...]\nand adds package management and\n[...]\nResourceLoader API\n[...]\n> - npm\n[...]\n@version is pinned\n> - git with @tag or @commit is pinned\n[...]\n> - pinned sources do not update\n[...]\n> - no ref means update pulls latest\n> - npm: and git: are only valid for extensions\n[...]\n> - packages can provide extensions, skills, prompts, and themes via pi field, or via auto discovery if no pi field exists\n> - CLI npm or git extensions install into /tmp/pi-extensions/ for that run only, no settings changes\n[...]\n> - PackageManager is invoked by DefaultResourceLoader on reload and installs missing sources automatically\n[...]\n> - PackageManager\n[...]\npass parse for disable flags and CLI sources\n> - construct DefaultResourceLoader and call reload to load extensions and collect flags\n> -\n[...]\npass parse with extension flags, set runtime flag values on the extensions runtime\n> - no second reload needed because flags do not affect resources\n[...]\n> \n> AgentSession reload\n[...]\n> \n> - Add runner initializer as described in the original issue. AgentSession stores it.\n> - AgentSession.reload calls loader.reload, creates a new ExtensionRunner, updates skills, prompts, themes, context files, rebuilds system prompt, then emits session_start.\n> - /reload command and --watch trigger AgentSession.reload.\n> - watch should work in all modes by calling AgentSession.reload.\n[...]\n> Implementation steps\n> \n> 1. Introduce ResourceLoader interface and DefaultResourceLoader.\n> 2. Add PackageManager and npm or git resolve and install.\n> 3. Update settings manager to use arrays for skills, prompts, themes. Remove SkillsSettings and include ignore filters.\n> 4. Refactor extension loader to support pi field for prompts, themes, skills.\n> 5. Refactor sdk.ts to accept ResourceLoader and remove discovery logic.\n> 6. Update main.ts to use ResourceLoader, two pass parsing, and flag propagation.\n> 7. Add AgentSession.reload and runner initializer pattern. Update modes to register initializer.\n> 8. Add /reload and --watch.\n> 9. Update CLI args and help.\n> 10. Update docs and changelog.\n[...]\nAuto-reloading\n[...]\nreload` command gives","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi","title":"GitHub - earendil-works/pi: AI agent toolkit","content":"# Repository: earendil-works/pi\n\nAI agent toolkit: coding agent CLI, unified LLM API, TUI & web UI libraries, Slack bot, vLLM pods\n\n- Stars: 49920\n- Forks: 5918\n- Watchers: 190\n- Open issues: 55\n- Primary language: TypeScript\n- Languages: TypeScript (96.4%), JavaScript (2.9%), CSS (0.4%), Shell (0.2%), HTML, C, PowerShell\n- License: MIT License (MIT)\n- Default branch: main\n- Created: 2025-08-09T14:03:50Z\n- Last push: 2026-05-15T23:06:45Z\n- Contributors: 210 (top: badlogic, mitsuhiko, github-actions[bot], hjanuschka, aliou, markusylisiurunen, Perlence, nicobailon, cristinaponcela, ferologics)\n- Releases: 214\n- Latest release: v0.74.0 (2026-05-07T17:46:51Z)\n\n---\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n pi.dev domain graciously donated by\n \n exe.dev \n \n\n> New issues and PRs from new contributors are auto-closed by default. Maintainers review auto-closed issues daily. See CONTRIBUTING.md.\n\n---\n\n# Pi Agent Harness Mono Repo\n\nThis is the home of the pi agent harness project including our self extensible coding agent.\n\n- **@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent**: Interactive coding agent CLI\n- **@earendil-works/pi-agent-core**: Agent runtime with tool calling and state management\n- **@earendil-works/pi-ai**: Unified multi-provider LLM API (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, …)\n\nTo learn more about pi:\n\n- Visit pi.dev, the project website with demos\n- Read the documentation, but you can also ask the agent to explain itself\n\n## Share your OSS coding agent sessions\n\nIf you use pi or other coding agents for open source work, please share your sessions.\n\nPublic OSS session data helps improve coding agents with real-world tasks, tool use, failures, and fixes instead of toy benchmarks.\n\nFor the full explanation, see this post on X.\n\nTo publish sessions, use `badlogic/pi-share-hf`. Read its README.md for setup instructions. All you need is a Hugging Face account, the Hugging Face CLI, and `pi-share-hf`.\n\nYou can also watch this video, where I show how I publish my `pi-mono` sessions.\n\nI regularly publish my own `pi-mono` work sessions here:\n\n- badlogicgames/pi-mono on Hugging Face\n\n## All Packages\n\n| Package | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| **@earendil-works/pi-ai** | Unified multi-provider LLM API (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, etc.) |\n| **@earendil-works/pi-agent-core** | Agent runtime with tool calling and state management |\n| **@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent** | Interactive coding agent CLI |\n| **@earendil-works/pi-tui** | Terminal UI library with differential rendering |\n| **@earendil-works/pi-web-ui** | Web components for AI chat interfaces |\n\nFor Slack/chat automation and workflows see earendil-works/pi-chat.\n\n## Contributing\n\nSee CONTRIBUTING.md for contribution guidelines and AGENTS.md for project-specific rules (for both humans and agents).\n\n## Development\n\n```bash\nnpm install          # Install all dependencies\nnpm run build        # Build all packages\nnpm run check        # Lint, format, and type check\n./test.sh            # Run tests (skips LLM-dependent tests without API keys)\n./pi-test.sh         # Run pi from sources (can be run from any directory)\n```\n\n> **Note:** `npm run check` requires `npm run build` to be run first. The web-ui package uses `tsc` which needs compiled `.d.ts` files from dependencies.\n\n## License\n\nMIT","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/README.md","title":"pi/packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/README.md at main","content":"# File:\n[...]\nextensions/README.md\n[...]\n```md\n# Extension Examples\n\nExample extensions for pi-coding-agent.\n[...]\n```bash\n# Load an extension with --extension flag\npi --extension examples/extensions/permission-gate.ts\n\n# Or copy to extensions directory for auto-discovery\ncp permission-gate.ts ~/.pi/agent/extensions/\n```\n[...]\n## Writing Extensions\n[...]\nSee docs/extensions.md for full documentation.\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport type { ExtensionAPI } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\nimport { Type } from \"typebox\";\n[...]\nexport default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {\n  // Subscribe to lifecycle events\n  pi.on(\"tool_call\", async (event, ctx) => {\n    if (event.toolName === \"bash\" && event.input.command?.includes(\"rm -rf\")) {\n      const ok = await ctx.ui.confirm(\"Dangerous!\", \"Allow rm -rf?\");\n      if (!ok) return { block: true, reason: \"Blocked by user\" };\n    }\n  });\n\n  // Register custom tools\n  pi.registerTool({\n    name: \"greet\",\n    label: \"Greeting\",\n    description: \"Generate a greeting\",\n    parameters: Type.Object({\n      name: Type.String({ description: \"Name to greet\" }),\n    }),\n    async execute(toolCallId, params, onUpdate, ctx, signal) {\n      return {\n        content: [{ type: \"text\", text: `Hello, ${params.name}!` }],\n        details: {},\n      };\n    },\n  });\n\n  // Register commands\n  pi.registerCommand(\"hello\", {\n    description: \"Say hello\",\n    handler: async (args, ctx) => {\n      ctx.ui.notify(\"Hello!\", \"info\");\n    },\n  });\n}\n```","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md","title":"pi/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md at main - GitHub","content":"```md\n> pi can help you create pi packages. Ask it to bundle your extensions, skills, prompt templates, or themes.\n[...]\nPi packages bundle extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes so you can share them through npm or git. A package can declare resources in `package.json` under the `pi` key, or use conventional directories.\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:@foo/bar@1.0.0\npi install git:github.com/user/repo@v1\npi install https://github.com/user/repo  # raw URLs work too\npi install /absolute/path/to/package\npi install ./relative/path/to/package\n\npi remove npm:@foo/bar\npi list                     # show installed packages from settings\npi update                   # update pi and all non-pinned packages\npi update --extensions      # update all non-pinned packages only\npi update --self            # update pi only\npi update --self --force    # reinstall pi even if current\npi update npm:@foo/bar      # update one package\npi update --extension npm:@foo/bar\n[...]\nTo try a package without installing it, use `--extension` or `-e`. This installs to a temporary directory for the current run only:\n[...]\nThird party runtime dependencies belong in `dependencies` in `package.json`. Dependencies that do not register extensions, skills, prompt templates, or themes also belong in `dependencies`. When pi installs a package from npm or git, it runs `npm install`, so those dependencies are installed automatically.\n[...]\nPi bundles core packages for extensions and skills. If you import any of these, list them in `peerDependencies` with a `\"*\"` range and do not bundle them: `@earendil-works/pi-ai`, `@earendil-works/pi-agent-core`, `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`, `@earendil-works/pi-tui`, `typebox`.","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/jayshah5696/pi-agent-extensions/","title":"jayshah5696/pi-agent-extensions","content":"# Repository: jayshah5696/pi-agent-extensions\n[...]\nCollection of extensions for pi coding agent (sessions, ask_user, handoff)\n[...]\n# pi-agent-extensions\n[...]\nA collection of extensions for the pi coding agent.\n[...]\n| Extension | Type | Description | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **sessions** | Command | Quick session picker with `/sessions` | ✅ Stable |\n| **ask_user** | Tool | LLM can ask structured questions | ⚙️ Beta |\n| **handoff** | Command | Goal-driven context transfer `/handoff` | ✅ Stable |\n| **whimsical** | UI | Context-aware loading messages & exit | ✅ Stable |\n| **files** | Tool | Unified file browser & git integration | ✅ Stable |\n| **notify** | Tool | Desktop notifications via OSC 777 | ✅ Stable |\n| **context** | UI | Context breakdown dashboard | ✅ Stable |\n| **review** | Tool | Interactive code review system | ✅ Stable |\n| **loop** | Tool | Iterative execution loop | ✅ Stable |\n[...]\n| **todos** | Tool | File-based todo list management | ✅ Stable |\n[...]\n| **control** |\n[...]\n| Inter-session communication\n[...]\ncontrol | ⚙\n[...]\nTracker | Tracks directory changes\n[...]\n✅ Stable |\n[...]\n| **btw\n[...]\n✅ Stable |\n[...]\n✅ Stable |\n[...]\n| **powerline\n[...]\n| Custom powerline\n[...]\n### From npm (Recommended)\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:pi-agent-extensions\n```\n[...]\nAll extensions will be available immediately after installation.\n[...]\n### From Source (For Development)\n[...]\n```bash\n# Clone the repository\ngit clone https://github.com/jayshah5696/pi-agent-extensions.git\ncd pi-agent-extensions\n\n# Install globally\npi install .\n\n# Or install to specific project\ncd ~/your-project\npi install -l /path/to/pi-agent-extensions\n```\n[...]\n### Extensions not showing after install\n[...]\nIf you installed via `npm install` or `npm update`, the package won't be registered with Pi. You must use **Pi's package manager**:\n[...]\n```bash\n# Wrong (npm only - won't register with Pi)\nnpm install pi-agent-extensions\n\n# Correct (registers with Pi)\npi install npm:pi-agent-extensions\n```\n[...]\n### Verify installation\n[...]\nCheck that the package appears in your settings:\n[...]\n```bash\ncat ~/.pi/agent/settings.json | grep pi-agent-extensions\n```\n[...]\nshould see:\n[...]\n```json\n\"packages\": [\n  \"npm:pi-agent-extensions\",\n  ...\n]\n```\n[...]\n### Local development vs npm\n[...]\nWhen running Pi from the `pi-agent-extensions` directory, it loads **local** extensions (your development copy), not the npm-installed version. This is useful for development but can cause confusion.\n[...]\ntest the npm version, run Pi\n[...]\na different directory:\n[...]\n```bash\ncd ~/some\n[...]\n# Check: should show npm:pi-agent-extensions in [Extensions]\n[...]\n```bash\nnpm install\nnpm test","error":null},{"url":"https://pi.dev/docs/latest/packages","title":"Pi Packages - Pi Coding Agent","content":"pi can help you create pi packages. Ask it to bundle your extensions, skills, prompt templates, or themes.\n[...]\nPi packages bundle extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes so you can share them through npm or git. A package can declare resources in`package.json` under the`pi` key, or use conventional directories.\n[...]\nSecurity: Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions execute arbitrary code, and skills can instruct the model to perform any action including running executables. Review source code before installing third-party packages.\n[...]\n```\npi install npm:@foo/bar@1.0.0\npi install git:github.com/user/repo@v1\npi install https://github.com/user/repo  # raw URLs work too\npi install /absolute/path/to/package\npi install ./relative/path/to/package\n\npi remove npm:@foo/bar\npi list                     # show installed packages from settings\npi update                   # update pi and all non-pinned packages\npi update --extensions      # update all non-pinned packages only\npi update --self            # update pi only\npi update --self --force    # reinstall pi even if current\npi update npm:@foo/bar      # update one package\npi update --extension npm:@foo/bar\n\n```\n[...]\nBy default,`install` and`remove` write to global settings (`~/.pi/agent/settings.json`). Use`-l` to write to project settings (`.pi/settings.json`) instead. Project settings can be shared with your team, and pi installs any missing packages automatically on startup.\n[...]\nTo try a package without installing it, use`--extension` or`-e`. This installs to a temporary directory for the current run only:\n[...]\n```\npi -e npm:@foo/bar\npi -e git:github.com/user/repo\n[...]\nPi accepts three source types in settings and`pi install`.\n[...]\n```\nnpm:@scope/pkg@1.2.3\nnpm:pkg\n\n```\n[...]\n- Versioned specs are pinned and skipped by package updates (`pi update`,`pi update --extensions`).\n- Global installs use`npm install -g`.\n- Project installs go under`.pi/npm/`.\n- Set`npmCommand` in`settings.json` to pin npm package lookup and install operations to a specific wrapper command such as`mise` or`asdf`.\n[...]\n- Without`git:` prefix, only protocol URLs are accepted (`https://`,`http://`,`ssh://`,`git://`).\n- With`git:` prefix, shorthand formats are accepted, including`github.com/user/repo` and`git@github.com:user/repo`.\n- HTTPS and SSH URLs are both supported.\n- SSH URLs use your configured SSH keys automatically (respects`~/.ssh/config`).\n- For non-interactive runs (for example CI), you can set`GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` to disable credential prompts and set`GIT_SSH_COMMAND`(for example`ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=5`) to fail fast.\n- Refs pin the package and skip package updates (`pi update`,`pi update --extensions`).\n- Cloned to`~/.pi/agent/git/ / `(global) or`.pi/git/ / `(project).\n- Runs`npm install` after clone or pull if`package.json` exists.\n[...]\nAdd a`pi` manifest to`package.json` or use conventional directories. Include the`pi-package` keyword for discoverability.\n[...]\n```\n{\n  \"name\": \"my-package\",\n  \"keywords\": [\"pi-package\"],\n  \"pi\": {\n    \"extensions\": [\"./extensions\"],\n    \"skills\": [\"./skills\"],\n    \"prompts\": [\"./prompts\"],\n    \"themes\": [\"./themes\"]\n  }\n}\n\n```\n[...]\nThird party runtime dependencies belong in`dependencies` in`package.json`. Dependencies that do not register extensions, skills, prompt templates, or themes also belong in`dependencies`. When pi installs a package from npm or git, it runs`npm install`, so those dependencies are installed automatically.\n[...]\nPi bundles core packages for extensions and skills. If you import any of these, list them in`peerDependencies` with a`\"*\"` range and do not bundle them:`@earendil-works/pi-ai`,`@earendil-works/pi-agent-core`,`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`,`@earendil-works/pi-tui`,`typebox`.\n[...]\nUse`pi config` to enable or disable extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes from installed packages and local directories. Works for both global (`~/.pi/agent`) and project (`.pi/`) scopes.","error":null},{"url":"https://pi.dev/","title":"Pi Coding Agent","content":"Pi is a minimal terminal coding harness. Adapt Pi to your workflows, not the other way around. Customize Pi with extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes. Bundle them as Pi packages and share via npm or git.\n[...]\nPi ships with powerful defaults but skips features like sub-agents and plan mode. Ask Pi to build what you want, or install a package that does it your way.\n[...]\nFeatures that other agents bake in, you can build yourself. Extensions are TypeScript modules with access to tools, commands, keyboard shortcuts, events, and the full TUI.\n[...]\nDon't want to build it? Ask Pi to build it for you. Or install a package that does it your way. See the 50+ examples.\n[...]\nBundle extensions, skills, prompts, and themes as packages. Install from npm or git:\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@foo/pi-tools$ pi install git:github.com/badlogic/pi-doom`\n[...]\nSpawn Pi instances via tmux, or build your own with extensions, or install a package that does it your way.\n[...]\nWrite plans to files, or build it with extensions, or install a package.\n[...]\nGitHub for bugs and features.\n[...]\nRead the docs or browse the source on GitHub.","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md","title":"packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md at main · earendil-works/pi","content":"```md\n> pi can help you create pi packages. Ask it to bundle your extensions, skills, prompt templates, or themes.\n\n#\n[...]\nPi packages bundle extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes so you can share them through npm or git. A package can declare resources in `package.json` under the `pi` key, or use conventional directories.\n[...]\n> **Security:** Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions execute arbitrary code, and skills can instruct the model to perform any action including running executables. Review source code before installing third-party packages.\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:@foo/bar@1.0.0\npi install git:github.com/user/repo@v1\npi install https://github.com/user/repo  # raw URLs work too\npi install /absolute/path/to/package\npi install ./relative/path/to/package\n\npi remove npm:@foo/bar\npi list                     # show installed packages from settings\npi update                   # update pi and all non-pinned packages\npi update --extensions      # update all non-pinned packages only\npi update --self            # update pi only\npi update --self --force    # reinstall pi even if current\npi update npm:@foo/bar      # update one package\npi update --extension npm:@foo/bar\n[...]\nBy default, `install` and `remove` write to global settings (`~/.pi/agent/settings.json`). Use `-l` to write to project settings (`.pi/settings.json`) instead. Project settings can be shared with your team, and pi installs any missing packages automatically on startup.\n[...]\nTo try a package without installing it, use `--extension` or `-e`. This installs to a temporary directory for the current run only:\n[...]\n```bash\npi -e npm:@foo/bar\npi -e git:github.com/user/repo\n[...]\nPi accepts three source types in settings and `pi install`.\n\n### npm\n[...]\n```\nnpm:@scope/pkg@1.2.3\nnpm:pkg\n```\n[...]\n- Versioned specs are pinned and skipped by package updates (`pi update`, `pi update --extensions`).\n- Global installs use `npm install -g`.\n- Project installs go under `.pi/npm/`.\n- Set `npmCommand` in `settings.json` to pin npm package lookup and install operations to a specific wrapper command such as `mise` or `asdf`.\n[...]\n- Without `git:` prefix, only protocol URLs are accepted (`https://`, `http://`, `ssh://`, `git://`).\n- With `git:` prefix, shorthand formats are accepted, including `github.com/user/repo` and `\n[...]\nconfigured SSH keys\n[...]\n(respects `~/.ssh/config\n[...]\n(for example\n[...]\nAL_PROMPT=0` to disable credential\n[...]\n- Refs pin the package and skip package\n[...]\n`, `pi\n[...]\n/.pi/agent\n[...]\n`npm install`\n[...]\nclone or pull\n[...]\n` exists.\n[...]\n## Creating a Pi Package\n[...]\nAdd a `pi` manifest to `package.json` or use conventional directories. Include the `pi-package` keyword for discoverability.\n[...]\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"my-package\",\n  \"keywords\": [\"pi-package\"],\n  \"pi\": {\n    \"extensions\": [\"./extensions\"],\n    \"skills\": [\"./skills\"],\n    \"prompts\": [\"./prompts\"],\n    \"themes\": [\"./themes\"]\n  }\n}\n[...]\nThird party runtime dependencies belong in `dependencies` in `package.json`. Dependencies that do not register extensions, skills, prompt templates, or themes also belong in `dependencies`. When pi installs a package from npm or git, it runs `npm install`, so those dependencies are installed automatically.\n[...]\nPi bundles core packages for extensions and skills. If you import any of these, list them in `peerDependencies` with a `\"*\"` range and do not bundle them: `@earendil-works/pi-ai`, `@earendil-works/pi-agent-core`, `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`, `@earendil-works/pi-tui`, `typebox`.\n[...]\nOther pi packages must be bundled in your tarball. Add them to `dependencies` and `bundledDependencies`, then reference their resources through `\n[...]\n_modules/` paths. Pi loads packages with separate module roots\n[...]\nUse `pi config` to enable or disable extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes from installed packages and local directories. Works for both global (`~/.pi/agent`) and project (`.pi/`) scopes.","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/hjanuschka/shitty-extensions?tab=readme-ov-file","title":"hjanuschka/shitty-extensions","content":"# Repository: hjanuschka/shitty-extensions\n[...]\nCustom hooks for pi coding agent\n[...]\n# shitty-extensions\n[...]\nCustom extensions and skills for pi coding agent.\n[...]\n**Requires pi 0.50.0+** | npm | GitHub\n[...]\n### Via pi install (recommended)\n[...]\nInstall from npm:\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:shitty-extensions\n```\n[...]\nOr install from git:\n[...]\n```bash\npi install git:github.com/hjanuschka/shitty-extensions\n```\n[...]\nThat's it! Pi automatically discovers all extensions and skills from the package.\n[...]\n### Install only clipboard extension\n[...]\nIf you only want the clipboard tool as a standalone package:\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:shitty-clipboard-extension\n```\n[...]\n### Try without installing\n[...]\nLoad for a single session without permanent installation:\n[...]\n```bash\npi -e npm:shitty-extensions\n```\n[...]\n### Project-local installation\n[...]\nInstall to `.pi/` for team sharing (auto-installs for teammates on startup):\n[...]\n```bash\npi install -l npm:shitty-extensions\n```\n[...]\n### Manual installation\n[...]\nClone the repo and reference directly:\n[...]\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/hjanuschka/shitty-extensions.git ~/shitty-extensions\npi -e ~/shitty-extensions\n```\n[...]\n## Available Extensions\n[...]\nExtensions are located in the `extensions/` directory.\n[...]\n0.52.13+\n[...]\n### clipboard.ts\n[...]\n### memory-mode.ts\n[...]\n## Available Skills\n[...]\n## Directory Structure\n[...]\nThis package follows the pi package conventions:\n[...]\n```\nshitty-extensions/\n├── package.json         # Declares extensions in \"pi\" field + \"pi-package\" keyword\n├── extensions/          # Auto-discovered extensions (.ts files)\n│   ├── branch-sessions.ts\n│   ├── clipboard.ts\n│   ├── cost-tracker.ts\n│   ├── flicker-corp.ts\n│   ├── funny-working-message.ts\n│   ├── handoff.ts\n│   ├── loop.ts\n│   ├── memory-mode.ts\n│   ├── oracle.ts\n│   ├── plan-mode.ts\n│   ├── resistance.ts\n│   ├── speedreading.ts\n│   ├── status-widget.ts\n│   ├── ultrathink.ts\n│   └── usage-bar.ts\n├── skills/              # Auto-discovered skills (SKILL.md folders)\n│   └── a-nach-b/\n│       ├── SKILL.md     # Skill definition & API docs\n│       ├── search.sh\n│       ├── departures.sh\n│       ├── route.sh\n│       └── disruptions.sh\n└── README.md\n```","error":null},{"url":"https://pi.dev/packages","title":"Package Catalog - Pi Coding Agent","content":"claude-code-tmuxDurable tmux controller for interactive Claude Code sessions, with a Pi extension and skill.2m ago pi-git-commandsPi agent extension: /git-commit and /git-push with LLM-generated Conventional Commits messages6m ago my-pi-themes14 custom themes for Pi Coding Agent: neapple, monokai-pro, onedark, e-ink, tokyo-dark, and more6m ago pi-cyber-uiStandalone Pi UI package with a cyber-inspired theme, custom editor, footer, and lightweight working indicator.14m ago @narumitw/pi-subagentsPi extension for delegating work to specialized isolated subagents.19m ago @narumitw/pi-statuslinePi extension that replaces the footer with an information-rich statusline.19m ago @narumitw/pi-retryPublic pi extension that retries empty-detail provider unknown errors.19m ago @narumitw/pi-python-lspPi extension that exposes ty and Ruff language-server tools for Python.19m ago\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:context-mode` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-subagents` Copy\n[...]\nMCP (Model Context Protocol) adapter extension for Pi coding agent\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-mcp-adapter` Copy\n[...]\nWeb search, URL fetching, GitHub repo cloning, PDF extraction, YouTube video understanding, and local video analysis for Pi coding agent\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-web-access` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@ollama/pi-web-search` Copy\n[...]\n### @plannotator/pi-extension\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@plannotator/pi-extension` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@juicesharp/rpiv-todo` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-simplify` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-lens` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-powerline-footer` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@juicesharp/rpiv-advisor` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@juicesharp/rpiv-web-tools` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@juicesharp/rpiv-btw` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-markdown-preview` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@juicesharp/rpiv-pi` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@runfusion/fusion` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:glimpseui` Copy\n[...]\n### @tintinweb/pi-subagents\n[...]\nA pi extension extension that brings smart Claude Code-style autonomous sub-agents to pi.\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@tintinweb/pi-subagents` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@samfp/pi-memory` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@juicesharp/rpiv-args` Copy\n[...]\n### pi-agent-flow\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-agent-flow` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-btw` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@gotgenes/pi-permission-system` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-ask-user` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@aliou/pi-processes` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@ff-labs/pi-fff` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-crew` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:taskplane` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@companion-ai/feynman` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@llblab/pi-telegram` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-chrome` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-studio` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-prompt-template-model` Copy\n[...]\n### pi-lean-ctx\n[...]\nPi Coding Agent extension (CLI-first) — routes bash/read/grep/find/ls through lean-ctx CLI for strong token savings. Optional MCP bridge can register advanced tools.\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-lean-ctx` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-hermes-memory` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-smart-fetch` Copy\n[...]\n### pi-depo\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-depo` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@astrofoundry/pi-astro` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@tmustier/pi-ralph-wiggum` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-schedule-prompt` Copy\n[...]\npi-intercom\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-intercom` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@heart-of-gold/toolkit` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@leing2021/super-pi` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@aliou/pi-guardrails` Copy\n[...]\n### pi-agent-browser-native\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-agent-browser-native` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:pi-convex` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:whatsapp-pi` Copy\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@juicesharp/rpiv-i18n` Copy\n[...]\n### @kaiserlich-dev/pi-session-search\n[...]\n`$ pi install npm:@kaiserlich-dev/pi-session-search` Copy","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/tmustier/pi-extensions","title":"tmustier/pi-extensions","content":"# Repository: tmustier/pi-extensions\n[...]\nA set of delightful extensions for Pi\n[...]\n- Stars: 313\n- Forks: 23\n- Watchers: 0\n- Open issues: 7\n- Primary language: TypeScript\n- Languages: TypeScript (70.9%), JavaScript (25.3%), Rust (1.7%), Python (1.5%), Shell (0.6%)\n- License: MIT License (MIT)\n- Topics: extension, npm, pi, pi-coding-agent\n- Default branch: main\n- Homepage: https://www.mustier.ai/projects/pi-extensions\n- Created: 2026-01-06T14:58:20Z\n- Last push: 2026-05-13T10:42:37Z\n- Contributors: 4 (top: tmustier, nicobailon, fernandezbaptiste, ttttmr)\n- Releases: 35\n- Latest release: session-recap/v0.1.3 (2026-05-12T12:02:31Z)\n[...]\n# pi-extensions\n[...]\nPersonal extensions for the Pi coding agent.\n[...]\n| Extension | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| /readfiles | In-terminal file browser and viewer widget. Navigate files, view diffs, select code, send comments to agent - without leaving Pi, and without interrupting your agent |\n| tab-status | Manage as many parallel sessions as your mind can handle. Terminal tab indicators for ✅ done / 🚧 stuck / 🛑 timed out |\n| pi-ralph-wiggum | Run arbitrarily-long tasks without diluting model attention. Flat version without subagents like ralph-loop |\n| agent-guidance | Switch between Claude/Codex/Gemini with model-specific guidance (CLAUDE.md, CODEX.md, GEMINI.md) |\n| /usage | 📊 Usage statistics dashboard. See cost, tokens, and messages by provider/model across Today, This Week, Last Week, and All Time — with a compact view for narrow terminals |\n| /paste | Paste editable text, not [paste #1 +21 lines]. Running `/paste` with optional keybinding |\n| /code | Pick code blocks or inline snippets from assistant messages to copy, insert, or run with `/code` |\n| session-recap | One-line recap above the editor when you refocus the terminal (or after idle). Keeps you in flow while multi-clauding |\n| arcade | Play minigames while your tests run: 👾 sPIce-invaders, 👻 picman, 🏓 ping, 🧩 tetris, 🍄 mario-not |\n[...]\n## Install (pi package manager)\n[...]\n```bash\npi install git:github.com/tmustier/pi-extensions\n```\n[...]\nTo enable only a subset, replace the package entry in `~/.pi/agent/settings.json` with a filtered one:\n[...]\n```json\n{\n  \"packages\": [\n    {\n      \"source\": \"git:github.com/tmustier/pi-extensions\",\n      \"extensions\": [\"files-widget/index.ts\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n[...]\n## Quick Setup\n[...]\nIf you keep a local clone, add extensions to your `~/.pi/agent/settings.json`:\n[...]\n```json\n{\n  \"extensions\": [\n    \"~/pi-extensions/files-widget\",\n    \"~/pi-extensions/tab-status/tab-status.ts\",\n    \"~/pi-extensions/arcade/spice-invaders.ts\",\n    \"~/pi-extensions/arcade/ping.ts\",\n    \"~/pi-extensions/arcade/picman.ts\",\n    \"~/pi-extensions/arcade/tetris.ts\",\n    \"~/pi-extensions/arcade/mario-not/mario-not.ts\",\n    \"~/pi-extensions/pi-ralph-wiggum\",\n    \"~/pi-extensions/agent-guidance/agent-guidance.ts\",\n    \"~/pi-extensions/raw-paste\",\n    \"~/pi-extensions/code-actions\",\n    \"~/pi-extensions/session-recap\",\n    \"~/pi-extensions/usage-extension\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n[...]\nFor agent-guidance, also run the setup script:\n[...]\n```bash\ncd ~/pi-extensions/agent-guidance && ./setup.sh\n```","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/refs/heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md","title":"heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md at refs · badlogic/pi-mono","content":"Pi is a minimal terminal coding harness. Adapt pi to your workflows, not the other way around, without having to fork and modify pi internals. Extend it with TypeScript Extensions, Skills, Prompt Templates, and Themes. Put your extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes in Pi Packages and share them with others via npm or git.\n[...]\n```bash\nnpm install -g @mariozechner/pi-coding-agent\n```\n[...]\nThen just talk to pi. By default, pi gives the model four tools: `read`, `write`, `edit`, and `bash`. The model uses these to fulfill your requests. Add capabilities via skills, prompt templates, extensions, or pi packages.\n[...]\n### Extensions\n\n \n\nTypeScript modules that extend pi with custom tools, commands, keyboard shortcuts, event handlers, and UI components.\n[...]\n### Pi Packages\n[...]\nBundle and share extensions, skills, prompts, and themes via npm or git. Find packages on npmjs.com or Discord.\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:@foo/pi-tools\npi install npm:@foo/pi-tools@1.2.3      # pinned version\n[...]\npi install git:github.com/user/repo\npi install git:github.com/user/repo@v1  # tag or commit\npi install git:git@github.com:user/repo\npi install git:git@github.com:user/repo@v1  # tag or commit\n[...]\npi install https://github.com/user/repo\npi install https://github.com/user/repo@v1      # tag or commit\n[...]\npi install ssh://git@github.com/user/repo\npi install ssh://git@github.com/user/repo@v1    # tag or commit\n[...]\npi update                               # update pi and packages (skips pinned packages)\npi update --extensions                  # update packages only\npi update --self                        # update pi only\npi update --self --force                # reinstall pi even if current\npi update npm:@foo/pi-tools             # update one package\npi config                               # enable/disable extensions, skills, prompts, themes\n[...]\nPackages install to `~/.pi/agent/git/` (git) or global npm. Use `-l` for project-local installs (`.pi/git/`, `.pi/npm/`). Git packages install dependencies with `npm install --omit=dev` by default, so runtime deps must be listed under `dependencies`; when `npmCommand` is configured, git packages use plain `install` for compatibility with wrappers. If you use a Node version manager and want package installs to reuse a stable npm context, set `npmCommand` in `settings.json`, for example `[\"mise\", \"exec\", \"node@20\", \"--\", \"npm\"]`.\n[...]\nCreate a package by adding a `pi` key to `package.json`:\n[...]\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"my-pi-package\",\n  \"keywords\": [\"pi-package\"],\n  \"pi\": {\n    \"extensions\": [\"./extensions\"],\n    \"skills\": [\"./skills\"],\n    \"prompts\": [\"./prompts\"],\n    \"themes\": [\"./themes\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n[...]\nWithout a `pi` manifest, pi auto-discovers from conventional directories (`extensions/`, `skills/`, `prompts/`, `themes/`).\n[...]\n```bash\npi install <source> [-l]     # Install package, -l for project-local\npi remove <source> [-l]      # Remove package\npi uninstall <source> [-l]   # Alias for remove\n[...]\npi update [source|self|pi]   # Update pi and packages (skips pinned packages)\npi update --extensions       # Update packages only\npi update --self             # Update pi only\npi update --self --force     # Reinstall pi even if current\npi update --extension <src>  # Update one package\npi list                      # List installed packages\npi config                    # Enable/disable package resources\n[...]\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| `-e`, `--extension ` | Load extension from path, npm, or git (repeatable) |\n| `--no-extensions` | Disable extension discovery |\n| `--skill ` | Load skill (repeatable) |\n| `--no-skills` | Disable skill discovery |\n| `--prompt-template ` | Load prompt template (repeatable) |\n| `--no-prompt-templates` | Disable prompt template discovery |\n| `--theme ` | Load theme (repeatable) |\n| `--no-themes` | Disable theme discovery |\n| `--no-context-files`, `-nc` | Disable AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md context file discovery |","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md","title":"pi/packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md at main - GitHub","content":"earendil\n[...]\nRepository: earendil-\n[...]\nLLM API, T\n[...]\n- Branch: main\n[...]\n```md\n> pi can create extensions\n[...]\nAsk it to build one for your use case.\n[...]\n> **Placement for /reload:** Put extensions in `~/.pi/agent/extensions/` (global) or `.pi/extensions/` (project-local) for auto-discovery. Use `pi\n[...]\ne ./path.ts` only for quick tests. Extensions in auto-discovered locations can be hot-reloaded with `/reload`.\n[...]\n```json\n{\n  \"packages\": [\n    \"npm:@foo/bar@1.0.0\",\n    \"git:github.com/user/repo@v1\"\n  ],\n  \"extensions\": [\n    \"/path/to/local/extension.ts\",\n    \"/path/to/local/extension/dir\"\n  ]\n}\n[...]\nTo share extensions via npm or git as pi packages, see packages.md.\n[...]\n## Available Imports\n[...]\n| Package | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` | Extension types (`ExtensionAPI`, `ExtensionContext`, events) |\n| `typebox` | Schema definitions for tool parameters |\n| `@earendil-works/pi-ai` | AI utilities (`StringEnum` for Google-compatible enums) |\n| `@earendil-works/pi-tui` | TUI components for custom rendering |\n[...]\nnpm dependencies work too. Add a `package.json` next to your extension (or in a parent directory), run `npm install`, and imports from `node_modules/` are resolved automatically.\n[...]\nFor distributed pi packages installed with `pi install` (npm or git), runtime deps must be in `dependencies`. Package installation uses production installs (`npm install --omit=dev`) by default, so `devDependencies` are not available at runtime; when `npmCommand` is configured, git packages use plain `install` for compatibility with wrappers.\n[...]\n**Package with dependencies** - for extensions that need npm packages:\n[...]\n```\n~/.pi/agent/extensions/\n└── my-extension/\n    ├── package.json    # Declares dependencies and entry points\n    ├── package-lock.json\n    ├── node_modules/   # After npm install\n    └── src/\n        └── index.ts\n```\n[...]\n```json\n// package.json\n{\n  \"name\": \"my-extension\",\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"zod\": \"^3.0.0\",\n    \"chalk\": \"^5.0.0\"\n  },\n  \"pi\": {\n    \"extensions\": [\"./src/index.ts\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n[...]\nRun `npm install` in the extension directory, then imports from `node_modules/` work automatically.","error":null},{"url":"https://www.bitdoze.com/pi-coding-agent-setup-guide/","title":"Pi Coding Agent Setup Guide: Install, Configure Models, and Best Extensions","content":"I have been running OpenCode and Hermes Agent for a while now, but Pi kept coming up in conversations. People on Reddit and Hacker News kept calling it \"the minimal one that does not get in your way.\" After two weeks of daily use, I see why. Pi is a terminal coding agent built by Mario Zechner that stays small at the core. You install it, point it at a project, and start working. Everything else — memory, MCP support, sub-agents, themes, skills — gets added through extensions you actually choose.\n[...]\nInstalling Pi on Linux, macOS, and via npm\n[...]\nPi is a terminal coding agent that reads your codebase, plans changes, edits files, runs shell commands, and iterates on failures. The default installation gives you four tools: read, write, edit, and bash. That is it. No plan mode, no MCP support, no memory, no sub-agents. You add those through extensions.\n[...]\nThe extension system is TypeScript. You drop a `.ts` file into `~/.pi/agent/extensions/` and Pi loads it. Extensions can register custom tools the LLM can call, intercept tool calls before they execute (to block `rm -rf` for example), add slash commands, modify the system prompt, and build custom TUI components. You can hot-reload them with `/reload` without restarting Pi.\n[...]\n## Installing Pi\n[...]\nPi is distributed as an npm package.\n[...]\n```bash\nnpm install -g @mariozechner/pi-coding-agent\n\n```\n[...]\nPi needs Node.js 18+. If you do not have Node, install it first:\n[...]\n):/workspace -w /workspace node:2\n[...]\nnpx @mariozechner/pi-coding\n[...]\n## Extensions worth installing\n[...]\npi-memory-md — Persistent memory stored as Markdown files. The agent remembers what it learned about your project across sessions. Without this, Pi starts fresh every time. This is the first extension I install.\n[...]\npi-mcp-adapter — Connects Pi to any MCP-compatible tool server. GitHub, Playwright, Brave Search, Postgres, Notion, Slack — any MCP server works. If you use MCP with other agents like Hermes or OpenClaw, this lets you reuse the same tools.\n[...]\nagents — Run isolated\n[...]\n-agents for\n[...]\nwork. When\n[...]\nsub-agents\n[...]\nthem simultaneously instead of doing everything in sequence.\n[...]\npi-plan — Read-only\n[...]\nmode with approval-based execution. Like Open\n[...]\n's Tab-to-switch\n[...]\nmode. Pi describes what it would do, you review, then approve. Safer for production code.\n[...]\npi-web-access — Web search and URL fetching inside Pi.\n[...]\nagent can look up\n[...]\n, check Stack\n[...]\n, or fetch API\n[...]\nwithout leaving the terminal\n[...]\npi-autoresearch — Long-running\n[...]\nuntil it has\n[...]\npi-simplify —\n[...]\nrecently changed code for\n[...]\n### Installing extensions\n[...]\nSingle-file extensions go directly in the extensions directory:\n[...]\nDirectory extensions:\n[...]\n```bash\n# Copy the directory\ncp -r pi-memory-md ~/.pi/agent/extensions/\n[...]\n# Install npm dependencies if the extension has a package.json\ncd ~/.pi/agent/extensions/pi-memory-md\nnpm install\n\n```\n[...]\nqvist is a curated collection of personal Pi skills and extensions. It is not meant to be installed as one package — browse the repo and copy what you need.\n[...]\n`bash-guard` —\n[...]\nthey run\n- `context.ts` — loads extra context from external sources\n- `filechanges` — tracks file modifications across turns\n- `google-image-search` — search Google Images from inside Pi\n- `memory.ts` — another persistent memory implementation\n- `subagents` — sub-agent support\n- `web-fetch` — fetch and read web pages\n- `web-search` — web\n[...]\nintegration\n- `youtube-search` — search YouTube from inside Pi\n- `video-extract` — extract information from video URLs\n- `zz-read-only-mode` — forces read-only mode (blocks all writes)\n[...]\n| --- | ---\n[...]\nInstall | npm package | npm\n[...]\n75+\n[...]\n`) is a\n[...]\nthat adds 6\n[...]\n, 7\n[...]\n, persistent memory, usage tracking, and\n[...]\nto Pi. It\n[...]\nEverything it installs lives\n[...]\nyour `~/.pi/agent/` directory and works","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/tintinweb/pi-subagents","title":"GitHub - tintinweb/pi-subagents: Sub-agents for pi with Claude Code ...","content":"# Repository: tintinweb/pi-subagents\n[...]\nSub-agents for pi with Claude Code look and feel — parallel execution, live widget, custom agent types, mid-run steering and more ...\n[...]\n# @tintinweb/pi-subagents\n[...]\nA pi extension that brings **Claude Code-style autonomous sub-agents** to pi. Spawn specialized agents that run in isolated sessions — each with its own tools, system prompt, model, and thinking level. Run them in foreground or background, steer them mid-run, resume completed sessions, and define your own custom agent types.\n[...]\n- **Claude Code look & feel** — same tool names, calling conventions, and UI patterns (`Agent`, `get_subagent_result`, `steer_subagent`) — feels native\n[...]\n- **Parallel background agents\n[...]\nsmart group join\n[...]\npersistent above-editor\n[...]\nanimated spinners,\n[...]\ntool activity, token counts, and colored status icons\n[...]\n- **Custom agent types** — define agents in `.pi/agents/.md` with YAML frontmatter: custom system prompts, model selection, thinking levels, tool restrictions\n[...]\n- **Mid-run steering** — inject messages into running agents to redirect their work without restarting\n[...]\n- **Event bus** — lifecycle events (`subagents:created`, `started`, `completed`, `failed`, `steered`, `compacted`) emitted\n[...]\n- **Cross-extension RPC** — other pi extensions can spawn and stop subagents via the `pi.events` event bus (`subagents:rpc:ping`, `subagents:rpc:spawn`, `subagents:rpc:stop`). Standardized reply envelopes with protocol versioning. Emits `subagents:ready` on load\n[...]\n## Custom Agents\n[...]\n-level agents\n[...]\nglobal ones with the same name, so you can customize a global agent\n[...]\n. The global location follows the upstream `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR` env var — set it to relocate all pi-coding-\n[...]\nstate (agents, skills, settings) to a custom directory.\n[...]\n| Field | Default | Description |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `description` | filename | Agent description shown in tool listings |\n| `display_name` | — | Display name for UI (e.g. widget, agent list) |\n| `tools` | all 7 | Comma-separated built-in tools: read, bash, edit, write, grep, find, ls. `none` for no tools |\n| `extensions` | `true` | Inherit MCP/extension tools. `false` to disable |\n| `skills` | `true` | Inherit skills from parent. Can be a comma-separated list of skill names to preload (see Skill Preloading for discovery locations) |\n[...]\n| `memory` |\n[...]\n## Cross-Extension RPC\n[...]\ncan spawn and stop\n[...]\nprogrammatically via the `pi.events` event bus, without\n[...]\n```\nsrc/\n  index.ts            # Extension entry: tool/command registration, rendering\n  types.ts            # Type definitions (AgentConfig, AgentRecord, etc.)\n  default-agents.ts   # Embedded default agent configs (general-purpose, Explore, Plan)\n  agent-types.ts      # Unified agent registry (defaults + user), tool name resolution\n  agent-runner.ts     # Session creation, execution, graceful max_turns, steer/resume\n  agent-manager.ts    # Agent lifecycle, concurrency queue, completion notifications\n  cross-extension-rpc.ts # RPC handlers for cross-extension spawn/ping via pi.events\n  group-join.ts       # Group join manager: batched completion notifications with timeout\n  custom-agents.ts    # Load user-defined agents from .pi/agents/*.md\n  memory.ts           # Persistent agent memory (resolve, read, build prompt blocks)\n  skill-loader.ts     # Preload skills (Pi-standard + Agent Skills spec layouts)\n  output-file.ts      # Streaming output file transcripts for agent sessions\n  worktree.ts         # Git worktree isolation (create, cleanup, prune)\n  prompts.ts          # Config-driven system prompt builder\n  context.ts          # Parent conversation context for inherit_context\n  env.ts              # Environment detection (git, platform)\n  ui/\n    agent-widget.ts       # Persistent widget: spinners, activity, status icons, theming\n    conversation-viewer.ts # Live conversation overlay for viewing agent sessions","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-subagents","title":"nicobailon/pi-subagents","content":"# Repository: nicobailon/pi-subagents\n[...]\nPi extension for async subagent delegation with truncation, artifacts, and session sharing\n[...]\n---\n\n \n \n \n\n# pi-subagents\n[...]\n`pi-subagents` lets Pi delegate work to focused child agents. Use it for code review, scouting, implementation, parallel audits, saved workflows, background jobs, and anything else that benefits from a second or third set of model eyes.\n[...]\nnpm:pi-\n[...]\nThe extension ships with builtin agents you can use immediately.\n[...]\n## Builtin agents in plain English\n[...]\n| Agent | Use it when you want... |\n| --- | --- |\n| `scout` | Fast local codebase recon: relevant files, entry points, data flow, risks, and where another agent should start. |\n| `researcher` | Web/docs research with sources: official docs, specs, benchmarks, recent changes, and a concise research brief. |\n| `planner` | A concrete implementation plan from existing context. It should read and plan, not edit code. |\n| `worker` | Implementation work, including approved oracle handoffs. It edits files, validates, and escalates unapproved decisions instead of guessing. |\n| `reviewer` | Code review and small fixes. It checks the implementation against the task/plan, tests, edge cases, and simplicity. |\n| `context-builder` | A stronger setup pass before planning: gathers code context and writes handoff material such as `context.md` and `meta-prompt.md`. |\n| `oracle` | A second opinion before acting. It challenges assumptions, catches drift, and recommends the safest next move without editing. |\n| `delegate` | A lightweight general delegate when you want a child agent that behaves close to the parent session. |\n[...]\n## Optional pi-intercom companion\n[...]\npi-subagents`\n[...]\n## Agents and chains\n[...]\n### Tool and extension selection\n[...]\nIf `tools` is omitted, `pi-subagents` does not pass `--tools`, so the child gets Pi’s normal builtin tools. If `tools` is present, regular tool names become an explicit allowlist. `mcp:` entries are split out and forwarded as direct MCP selections. Path-like `tools` entries, such as extension paths or `.ts`/`.js` files, are treated as tool-extension paths rather than builtin tool names.\n[...]\n`extensions` controls child extension loading:\n[...]\n### Bundled skill\n[...]\nThe package bundles a `pi-subagents` skill that is automatically available to the parent agent when the extension is installed. It is for the orchestrating parent only: child subagents never receive it, and their context is explicitly filtered to strip parent-only orchestration instructions.\n[...]\nasync mode, and whether\n[...]\n- **Prompt workflow recipes**: how to apply the packaged techniques directly with `subagent(...)` when the user describes the workflow in natural language instead of invoking a slash command. This includes parallel review, review-loop, parallel research, parallel context-build, parallel handoff-plan, gather-context-and-clarify, and parallel cleanup\n[...]\n- **Intercom conventions**: when to ask vs send, and how parent-side result delivery works with `pi-intercom`\n[...]\npatterns. If\n[...]\nneed to read\n[...]\n### `intercomBridge`\n[...]\n## Session sharing\n[...]\n: true` to\n[...]\nyour `gh`\n[...]\nshittyc\n[...]\n## Prompt-template integration\n[...]\n`pi-subagents` works standalone through natural language, the `subagent` tool, slash commands, and the packaged prompt shortcuts listed near the top of this README. If you use pi-prompt-template-model, you can also wrap subagent delegation in your own reusable prompt templates.\n[...]\n|\n| --- | --- |\n| `src/extension/index.ts` | Extension registration, tool registration, message/render wiring. |\n| `src/agents/agents.ts` | Agent and chain discovery, frontmatter parsing. |\n| `src/runs/foreground/subagent-executor.ts` | Main execution routing for single, parallel, chain, management, status, interrupt, and doctor actions. |\n| `src/runs/foreground/execution.ts` | Core foreground `runSync` handling\n[...]\n/background/sub\n[...]\n` | Detached","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/cmf/pi-subagent","title":"cmf/pi-subagent","content":"# Repository: cmf/pi-subagent\n[...]\n# pi-subagent\n[...]\nSubagent infrastructure for pi coding agent extensions. Spawn and orchestrate pi subprocesses with full observability.\n[...]\n```bash\nnpm install @cmf/pi-subagent\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport type { ExtensionAPI } from \"@mariozechner/pi-coding-agent\";\nimport { invokeAgentWithUI, registerSubagentRenderer } from \"@cmf/pi-subagent\";\n[...]\nexport default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {\n  registerSubagentRenderer(pi);\n\n  pi.registerCommand(\"research\", {\n    handler: async (args, ctx) => {\n      await invokeAgentWithUI(ctx, pi, {\n        step: {\n          chain: [\n            {\n              parallel: [\n                { cwd: ctx.cwd, agent: \"scout\", task: `Find docs: ${args}` },\n                { cwd: ctx.cwd, agent: \"scout\", task: `Find code: ${args}` },\n              ],\n            },\n            { cwd: ctx.cwd, agent: \"planner\", task: \"Summarize:\\n\\n{previous}\" },\n          ],\n        },\n      });\n    },\n  });\n}\n```\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport { invokeAgent } from \"@cmf/pi-subagent\";\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport {\n  invokeAgentWithUI,\n  registerSubagentRenderer,\n  createProgressComponent,\n  persistResults,\n} from \"@cmf/pi-subagent\";\n[...]\n### Agent Discovery\n[...]\n```typescript\nimport { discoverAgents } from \"@cmf/pi-subagent\";\n[...]\nconst { agents, projectAgentsDir } = discoverAgents(ctx.cwd, \"both\");\n// agents: AgentConfig[]\n// projectAgentsDir: string | null\n[...]\n## Peer Dependencies\n[...]\nRequires pi coding agent >= 0.38.0:\n[...]\n```json\n{\n  \"peerDependencies\": {\n    \"@mariozechner/pi-ai\": \">=0.38.0\",\n    \"@mariozechner/pi-coding-agent\": \">=0.38.0\",\n    \"@mariozechner/pi-tui\": \">=0.38.0\"\n  }\n}\n```","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/rylwin/pi-subagents","title":"rylwin/pi-subagents","content":"# Repository: rylwin/pi-subagents (fork of tintinweb/pi-subagents)\n[...]\nSub-agents for pi with Claude Code look and feel — parallel execution, live widget, custom agent types, mid-run steering and more ...\n[...]\n# @tintinweb/pi-subagents\n[...]\nA pi extension that brings **Claude Code-style autonomous sub-agents** to pi. Spawn specialized agents that run in isolated sessions — each with its own tools, system prompt, model, and thinking level. Run them in foreground or background, steer them mid-run, resume completed sessions, and define your own custom agent types.\n[...]\n- **Claude Code look & feel** — same tool names, calling conventions, and UI patterns (`Agent`, `get_subagent_result`, `steer_subagent`) — feels native\n[...]\n- **Parallel background agents**\n[...]\nspawn multiple agents that run concurrently with automatic queuing\n[...]\nlimit, default 4) and smart group join\n[...]\n- **Custom agent types** — define agents in `.pi/agents/.md` with YAML frontmatter: custom system prompts, model selection, thinking levels, tool restrictions\n[...]\n- **Mid-run steering** — inject messages into running agents to redirect their work without restarting\n[...]\n- **Event bus** — lifecycle events (`subagents:created`, `started`, `completed`, `failed`, `steered`, `compacted`) emitted\n[...]\n`pi.\n[...]\n-agent activity\n[...]\n- **Cross-extension RPC** — other pi extensions can spawn and stop subagents via the `pi.events` event bus (`subagents:rpc:ping`, `subagents:rpc:spawn`, `subagents:rpc:stop`). Standardized reply envelopes with protocol versioning. Emits `subagents:ready` on load\n[...]\nsubagents** — pass `\n[...]\n` to the `Agent`\n[...]\n`/agents →\n[...]\n## Custom Agents\n[...]\nProject-level agents override global ones with the same name, so you can customize a global agent for a specific project. The global location follows the upstream `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR` env var — set it to relocate all pi-coding-agent state (agents, skills, settings) to a custom directory.\n[...]\n| Default | Description |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `description` | filename | Agent description shown in tool listings |\n| `display_name` | — | Display name for UI (e.g. widget, agent list) |\n| `tools` | all 7 | Comma-separated built-in tools: read, bash, edit, write, grep, find, ls. `none` for no tools |\n| `extensions` | `true` | Inherit MCP/extension tools. `false` to disable |\n| `skills` | `true` | Inherit skills from parent. Can be a comma-separated list of skill names to preload from `.pi/skills/` |\n[...]\n| `memory` | — |\n[...]\n## Cross-Extension RPC\n[...]\nsrc/\n  index.ts            # Extension entry: tool/command registration, rendering\n  types.ts            # Type definitions (AgentConfig, AgentRecord, etc.)\n  default-agents.ts   # Embedded default agent configs (general-purpose, Explore, Plan)\n  agent-types.ts      # Unified agent registry (defaults + user), tool name resolution\n  agent-runner.ts     # Session creation, execution, graceful max_turns, steer/resume\n  agent-manager.ts    # Agent lifecycle, concurrency queue, completion notifications\n  cross-extension-rpc.ts # RPC handlers for cross-extension spawn/ping via pi.events\n  group-join.ts       # Group join manager: batched completion notifications with timeout\n  custom-agents.ts    # Load user-defined agents from .pi/agents/*.md\n  memory.ts           # Persistent agent memory (resolve, read, build prompt blocks)\n  skill-loader.ts     # Preload skill files from .pi/skills/\n  output-file.ts      # Streaming output file transcripts for agent sessions\n  worktree.ts         # Git worktree isolation (create, cleanup, prune)\n  prompts.ts          # Config-driven system prompt builder\n  context.ts          # Parent conversation context for inherit_context\n  env.ts              # Environment detection (git, platform)\n  ui/\n    agent-widget.ts       # Persistent widget: spinners, activity, status icons, theming\n    conversation-viewer.ts # Live conversation overlay for viewing agent sessions","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/mjakl/pi-subagent","title":"mjakl/pi-subagent","content":"# Repository: mjakl/pi-subagent\n[...]\nA lightweight subagent extension for the pi coding agent\n[...]\n# Pi Subagent\n[...]\n**Delegate tasks to specialized subagents with configurable context modes (`spawn` / `fork`).**\n\nThere are many subagent extensions for pi, this one is mine.\n[...]\n### Option 1: Install from npm (recommended)\n[...]\n```bash\npi install npm:@mjakl/pi-subagent\n```\n[...]\n### Option 2: Install via git\n[...]\n```bash\npi install git:github.com/mjakl/pi-subagent\n```\n[...]\n### Option 3: Manual Installation\n[...]\nClone this repository to your Pi extensions directory:\n[...]\n```bash\ncd ~/.pi/agent/extensions\ngit clone https://github.com/mjakl/pi-subagent.git\ncd pi-subagent\nnpm install\n```\n[...]\n### Context Mode (`spawn` vs `fork`)\n[...]\n### Subagent Definitions\n[...]\nfiles with YAML frontmatter.\n[...]\n**User Agents:** `~/.pi/agent/agents/*.md` by default, or `$PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR/agents/*.md` when `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR` is set\n[...]\n**Project Agents:** `.pi/agents/*.md`\n[...]\n`PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR` follows Pi's config-dir override semantics: when it is set, the extension uses `$PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR/agents` as the user/global agent directory instead of `~/.pi/agent/agents`. Project agents are still loaded in addition to the active user/global directory, and project agents win on name conflicts. When project agents are requested, Pi will prompt for confirmation before running them.\n[...]\n| Field |\n[...]\n| Default |\n[...]\n|\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n[...]\n| `name` | Yes | —\n[...]\n` | Yes | — | What the\n[...]\n) |\n| `model` | No | Uses the default\n[...]\nmodel | Overrides\n[...]\nprovider prefix (\n[...]\n.g. `anthropic/claude-3-5-sonnet` or `openrouter/claude-3.5-sonnet`) to force a specific provider. |\n| `thinking` | No | Uses Pi's default thinking level | Sets\n[...]\nthinking level (`off`, `minimal`, `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`). Equivalent to `--thinking\n[...]\n|\n| `tools` | No | `read,\n[...]\n` | Com\n[...]\nlist of **built-in**\n[...]\nto enable for\n[...]\n. If omitted, defaults apply\n[...]\n- **Auto-Discovery** — Agents are found at startup and their descriptions are injected into the main agent's system prompt.\n- **Context Mode Switch** — `spawn` (fresh context) and `fork` (session snapshot + task) per call.\n- **Depth + Cycle Guards** — Depth limiting and ancestry-cycle checks prevent runaway recursive delegation by default.\n- **Streaming Updates** — Watch subagent progress in real-time as tool calls and outputs stream in.\n- **Rich TUI Rendering** — Collapsed/expanded views with usage stats, tool call previews, and markdown output.\n- **Security Confirmation** — Project-local agents require explicit user approval before execution.\n[...]\n## Project Structure\n[...]\n```\nindex.ts       — Extension entry point: lifecycle hooks, tool registration, mode orchestration\nagents.ts      — Agent discovery: reads and parses .md files from the active Pi config dir and project directories\nrunner-cli.js  — Parent CLI inheritance: parses and normalizes flags forwarded to child processes\nrunner.ts      — Process runner: starts `pi` subprocesses in spawn/fork context modes and streams JSON events\nrender.ts      — TUI rendering: renderCall and renderResult for the subagent tool\ntypes.ts       — Shared types and pure helper functions\n```","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/dnouri/pi-coding-agent/issues/176","title":"Extension UI support for Emacs #176 - dnouri/pi-coding-agent - GitHub","content":"- Repository: dnouri/\n[...]\nfrontend for the\n[...]\n| Extension | UI mechanism | Emacs today |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `pi-interactive-subagents` | tmux / zellij / cmux pane layout | No Emacs surface defined |\n| `pi-glimpseui` | Native HTML window (WKWebView, macOS only) | Works via browser; `xwidget-webkit` would be native and cross-platform |\n| `pi-extensions` files-widget | `ctx.ui.custom()` file browser overlay | Silent fallback |\n| `pi-subagents` agents manager | `ctx.ui.custom()` keyboard-nav overlay | Silent fallback |\n| Powerline / sub-bar footers | Composite TUI footer components | Partially lost |\n[...]\n> > \n> > These **cannot be serialized over JSON RPC**. The extension code literally instantiates a JavaScript object graph with methods. This is the hard wall for extensions like `pi-extensions`' file browser and `pi-subagents`' agent manager — they use `ctx.ui.custom()` which returns `undefined` in RPC mode, and the extension gets nothing back.\n[...]\n> - permissions - e.g. anything configured to require confirmation with pi-guardrails is just silently run in emacs currently\n> \n> pi-sub-bar is nice in that it shows subscription limits but not as important as these. Might be nice to support subagent menus, but I use tintinweb/pi-subagents and have not yet actually needed the UI menu.\n[...]\n> Automated note: the table below is an **automated compatibility snapshot** based on web search / GitHub discovery plus source inspection of popular pi extensions' `ctx.ui.*` usage. It is meant as a **directional map**, not full end-to-end validation of every extension in Emacs.\n> \n> Using GitHub stars as a rough popularity proxy, the results seem to split into three groups:\n> \n> - extensions that already mostly work in Emacs today,\n> - extensions that would benefit from a small missing feature like `editor`, string-array `setWidget`, `setWorkingMessage`, or `setTitle`,\n> - and extensions whose main UX depends on rich TUI components (`ctx.ui.custom()`, component-backed `setWidget`, `setFooter`, `setHeader`, `setEditorComponent`), which look like the real protocol boundary.\n> \n> ### Legend\n> \n> - **\n[...]\nwork in Emacs\n[...]\n> | `nicobailon/pi-subagents` | 684 | Easy win / partial | string-array `setWidget`; later `custom()` | Live progress widget is data-only and should benefit immediately from `setWidget`; manager/clarify flows still use `custom()` |\n[...]\n> | `tintinweb/pi-subagents` | 126 | Easy win / partial | `editor`; optional `custom()` | A lot of management flow uses `select`/`input`/`confirm`/`editor`; one viewer remains custom |\n[...]\nsuggests\n>\n[...]\n> #### Already in decent shape\n> \n> These should mostly work today:\n> \n> - `pi-generative-ui`\n> - `pi-mcp-adapter` (core flow)\n> - `pi-web-access`\n[...]\ncore flow)\n> - `pi-\n[...]\n-pass` (core flow)\n> - `pi-\n[...]\nboomerang`\n[...]\ntelegram`\n> - `pi-\n[...]\nind-hook`\n> - `pi-review-loop`\n> - `pi-magic-docs`\n> - `pi-sub-status` (from `pi-sub`)\n> \n> #### Best small wins\n> \n> These look like the most valuable incremental additions:\n> \n> 1. **`editor`**\n> \n> - especially helps `tintinweb/pi-subagents`\n> - also helps parts of `shitty-extensions` and some bundle repos\n> \n> 1. **`setWidget` for string arrays**\n> \n> - especially helps `nicobailon/pi-subagents`\n> - also helps data-only widgets like plan/progress/task summaries\n> \n> 1. **`setWorkingMessage`**\n> \n> - tiny upstream fix\n> - modest payoff\n> \n> 1. **`setTitle`**\n> \n> - trivial\n> - nice for\n[...]\nstatus-style extensions, but low impact overall\n[...]\n> \n> #### Real hard boundary\n> \n> The biggest unsupported class still appears\n[...]\n> - `ctx.\n[...]\n.custom()`\n> - component/factory `set\n[...]\n`\n> - `setFooter()`\n> -\n[...]\nsetHeader()`\n> - `setEditorComponent()`\n> \n> That affects many of the most visible UI-heavy extensions:\n> \n> - `pi-autoresearch`\n> - `\n[...]\n-messenger`\n>\n[...]\n-shell`\n> - `\n[...]\n-interactive-subagents`\n> - `pi-autoresearch-studio`\n> - `\n[...]\n-powerline-footer`\n> - `\n[...]\n-guardrails`\n> - `pi-tasks`","error":null},{"url":"https://pi.dev/packages/pi-subagents","title":"pi-subagents - Pi Coding Agent","content":"Pi Coding Agent\n[...]\nInstall`pi-subagents` from npm and Pi will load the resources declared by the package manifest.\n[...]\nPackage`pi-subagents` Version`0.24.3` PublishedMay 15, 2026Downloads80.8K/mo · 23.7K/wkAuthornicopremeLicenseMITTypesextension, skill, promptSize962.9 KBDependencies2 dependencies · 4 peersPi manifest JSON\n[...]\n```\n{\n  \"extensions\": [\n    \"./src/extension/index.ts\"\n  ],\n  \"skills\": [\n    \"./skills\"\n  ],\n  \"prompts\": [\n    \"./prompts\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n[...]\n# pi-subagents\n[...]\n`pi-subagents` lets Pi delegate work to focused child agents. Use it for code review, scouting, implementation, parallel audits, saved workflows, background jobs, and anything else that benefits from a second or third set of model eyes.\n[...]\n```\npi install npm:pi-subagents\n\n```\n[...]\nwith builtin agents you can use immediately.\n[...]\n| Agent | Use it when you want... |\n| --- | --- |\n| `scout` | Fast local codebase recon: relevant files, entry points, data flow, risks, and where another agent should start. |\n| `researcher` | Web/docs research with sources: official docs, specs, benchmarks, recent changes, and a concise research brief. |\n| `planner` | A concrete implementation plan from existing context. It should read and plan, not edit code. |\n| `worker` | Implementation work, including approved oracle handoffs. It edits files, validates, and escalates unapproved decisions instead of guessing. |\n| `reviewer` | Code review and small fixes. It checks the implementation against the task/plan, tests, edge cases, and simplicity. |\n| `context-builder` | A stronger setup pass before planning: gathers code context and writes handoff material such as`context.md` and`meta-prompt.md`. |\n| `oracle` | A second opinion before acting. It challenges assumptions, catches drift, and recommends the safest next move without editing. |\n| `delegate` | A lightweight general delegate when you want a child agent that behaves close to the parent session. |\n[...]\n### Tool and extension selection\n[...]\nIf`tools` is omitted,`pi-subagents` does not pass`--tools`, so the child gets Pi’s normal builtin tools. If`tools` is present, regular tool names become an explicit allowlist.`mcp:` entries are split out and forwarded as direct MCP selections. Path-like`tools` entries, such as extension paths or`.ts`/`.js` files, are treated as tool-extension paths rather than builtin tool names.\n[...]\n`extensions` controls child extension loading:\n[...]\nWhen`extensions` is present, it takes precedence over extension paths implied by`tools` entries.\n[...]\n### Bundled skill\n[...]\nThe package bundles a`pi-subagents` skill that is automatically available to the parent agent when the extension is installed. It is for the orchestrating parent only: child subagents never receive it, and their context is explicitly filtered to strip parent-only orchestration instructions.\n[...]\n`~/.pi/\n[...]\n`pi-subagents`\n[...]\nwrap subagent\n[...]\n## Runtime files\n[...]\n| File | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `src/extension/index.ts` | Extension registration, tool registration, message/render wiring. |\n| `src/agents/agents.ts` | Agent and chain discovery, frontmatter parsing. |\n| `src/runs/foreground/subagent-executor.ts` | Main execution routing for single, parallel, chain, management, status, interrupt, and doctor actions. |\n| `src/runs/foreground/execution.ts` | Core foreground`runSync` handling. |\n| `src/runs/background/subagent-runner.ts` | Detached async runner. |\n| `src/runs/background/async-execution.ts` | Background launch support. |\n| `src/runs/background/async-status.ts` | Status discovery and formatting for async runs. |\n| `src\n[...]\nruns/foreground/chain-execution.ts`/`src/agents/chain-serializer.ts` | Chain orchestration and`.chain.md` parsing. |\n| `src/shared/settings.ts` |\n[...]\nbehavior, instructions, and config helpers\n[...]\n|\n| `src/runs/shared/worktree.ts` | Git worktree isolation. |\n| `src/intercom/intercom-bridge.ts` | Runtime intercom bridge instructions and diagnostics. |\n| `","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/johansja/pi-extensions","title":"johansja/pi-extensions","content":"# Repository: johansja/pi-extensions\n[...]\nVarious extensions for pi coding agent\n[...]\n# Pi Extensions\n[...]\nCustom extensions for pi, the coding agent harness.\n[...]\n> **Attribution:** These extensions are derived from example extensions in the pi-mono repository:\n> - **ai-permission-gate** evolved from pi-mono's `permission-gates` example — replacing regex-based pattern matching with LLM-powered classification and CWD-aware risk assessment.\n> - **team** evolved from pi-mono's `subagent` example — expanding from a simple subagent spawner into a full multi-role workflow orchestrator with phase tracking, file-based mailboxes, and auto-orchestration.\n\n## Extensions\n[...]\n### ai-permission-gate\n[...]\nOrchestrates multi-role development workflows across cmux panes. Each role (planner, coder, reviewer) runs as a full pi session with its own pane, model, tools, and file-based mailbox for inter-role communication.\n[...]\n**Workflow model:** LLM-driven orchestration. After `/team init`, you use the `team_orchestrate` tool to dispatch agents one at a time. The orchestrator (main pi session) decides what to run next based on agent results.\n[...]\n**Agent configuration:** Place `.md` files in `~/.pi/agent/team/` (user-level) or `.pi/team/` (project-level). Each file has YAML frontmatter:\n[...]\nai-permission-gate.\n[...]\n/ai-permission-gate\n[...]\n# team\nln -sf ~/projects/pi-extensions/team ~/.pi/agent/extensions/team\n[...]\n# Agent configuration for team (required)\nmkdir -\n[...]\n~/.pi/agent/team\n[...]\ncp ~/projects/pi-extensions/team/examples/*.md ~/.pi/\n[...]\n/team/","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/prisema/pi-subagents-tintinweb","title":"prisema/pi-subagents-tintinweb","content":"# Repository: prisema/pi-subagents-tintinweb (fork of tintinweb/pi-subagents)\n[...]\nSub-agents for pi with\n[...]\n— parallel execution, live widget, custom agent types, mid-run steering and more ...\n[...]\n# @tintinweb/pi-subagents\n[...]\nA pi extension that brings **Claude Code-style autonomous sub-agents** to pi. Spawn specialized agents that run in isolated sessions — each with its own tools, system prompt, model, and thinking level. Run them in foreground or background, steer them mid-run, resume completed sessions, and define your own custom agent types.\n[...]\n- **Claude Code look & feel** — same tool names, calling conventions, and UI patterns (`Agent`, `get_subagent_result`, `steer_subagent`) — feels native\n[...]\n- **Custom agent types** — define agents in `.pi/agents/.md` with YAML frontmatter: custom system prompts, model selection, thinking levels, tool restrictions\n[...]\n- **Mid-run steering** — inject messages into running agents to redirect their work without restarting\n[...]\n- **Event bus** — lifecycle events (`subagents:created`, `started`, `completed`, `failed`, `steered`) emitted via `pi.events`, enabling other extensions to react to sub-agent activity\n[...]\n- **Cross-extension RPC** — other pi extensions can spawn and stop subagents via the `pi.events` event bus (`subagents:rpc:ping`, `subagents:rpc:spawn`, `subagents:rpc:stop`). Standardized reply envelopes with protocol versioning. Emits `subagents:ready` on load\n[...]\nglobal ones with the\n[...]\nname, so you can\n[...]\na global agent\n[...]\n. The global\n[...]\nfollows the upstream `PI\n[...]\nvar — set it to\n[...]\nagents, skills, settings) to a custom directory.\n[...]\n| Field | Default | Description |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `description` | filename | Agent description shown in tool listings |\n| `display_name` | — | Display name for UI (e.g. widget, agent list) |\n| `tools` | all 7 | Comma-separated built-in tools: read, bash, edit, write, grep, find, ls. `none` for no tools |\n| `extensions` | `true` | Inherit MCP/extension tools. `false` to disable |\n| `skills` | `true` | Inherit skills from parent. Can be a comma-separated list of skill names to preload from `.pi/skills/` |\n| `memory` | — | Persistent agent memory scope: `project`, `local`, or `user`. Auto-detects read-only agents |\n| `\n[...]\nComma-separated tools to deny\n[...]\nif extensions provide them\n[...]\n## Cross-Extension\n[...]\nOther pi extensions can spawn and stop subagents programmatically via the `pi.events` event bus, without importing this package directly.\n[...]\n```\nsrc/\n  index.ts            # Extension entry: tool/command registration, rendering\n  types.ts            # Type definitions (AgentConfig, AgentRecord, etc.)\n  default-agents.ts   # Embedded default agent configs (general-purpose, Explore, Plan, Implement, Review)\n  agent-types.ts      # Unified agent registry (defaults + user), tool name resolution\n  agent-runner.ts     # Session creation, execution, graceful max_turns, steer/resume\n  agent-manager.ts    # Agent lifecycle, concurrency queue, completion notifications\n  cross-extension-rpc.ts # RPC handlers for cross-extension spawn/ping via pi.events\n  group-join.ts       # Group join manager: batched completion notifications with timeout\n  custom-agents.ts    # Load user-defined agents from .pi/agents/*.md\n  memory.ts           # Persistent agent memory (resolve, read, build prompt blocks)\n  skill-loader.ts     # Preload skill files from .pi/skills/\n  output-file.ts      # Streaming output file transcripts for agent sessions\n  worktree.ts         # Git worktree isolation (create, cleanup, prune)\n  prompts.ts          # Config-driven system prompt builder\n  context.ts          # Parent conversation context for inherit_context\n  env.ts              # Environment detection (git, platform)\n  ui/\n    agent-widget.ts       # Persistent widget: spinners, activity, status icons, theming\n    conversation-viewer.ts # Live conversation overlay for viewing agent sessions","error":null},{"url":"https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-subagents/blob/main/README.md","title":"README.md at main · nicobailon/pi-subagents","content":"- Repository: nicobailon/pi-subagents | Pi extension for async subagent delegation with truncation, artifacts, and session sharing | 1K stars | TypeScript\n- Branch: main\n[...]\n# pi-subagents\n[...]\n`pi-subagents` lets Pi delegate work to focused child agents. Use it for code review, scouting, implementation, parallel audits, saved workflows, background jobs, and anything else that benefits from a second or third set of model eyes.\n[...]\nbash\npi install npm:pi-sub\n[...]\nThe extension ships with builtin agents you can use immediately.\n[...]\n## Builtin agents in\n[...]\n| Agent | Use it when you want... |\n|-------|--------------------------|\n| `scout` | Fast local codebase recon: relevant files, entry points, data flow, risks, and where another agent should start. |\n| `researcher` | Web/docs research with sources: official docs, specs, benchmarks, recent changes, and a concise research brief. |\n| `planner` | A concrete implementation plan from existing context. It should read and plan, not edit code. |\n| `worker` | Implementation work, including approved oracle handoffs. It edits files, validates, and escalates unapproved decisions instead of guessing. |\n| `reviewer` | Code review and small fixes. It checks the implementation against the task/plan, tests, edge cases, and simplicity. |\n| `context-builder` | A stronger setup pass before planning: gathers code context and writes handoff material such as `context.md` and `meta-prompt.md`. |\n| `oracle` | A second opinion before acting. It challenges assumptions, catches drift, and recommends the safest next move without editing. |\n| `delegate` | A lightweight general delegate when you want a child agent that behaves close to the parent session. |\n[...]\n### Tool and extension selection\n[...]\nIf `tools` is omitted, `pi-subagents` does not pass `--tools`, so the child gets Pi’s normal builtin tools. If `tools` is present, regular tool names become an explicit allowlist. `mcp:` entries are split out and forwarded as direct MCP selections. Path-like `tools` entries, such as extension paths or `.ts`/`.js` files, are treated as tool-extension paths rather than builtin tool names.\n[...]\n`extensions` controls child\n[...]\n### Bundled skill\n[...]\nThe package bundles a `pi-subagents` skill that is automatically available to the parent agent when the extension is installed. It is for the orchestrating parent only: child subagents never receive it, and their context is explicitly filtered to strip parent-only orchestration instructions.\n[...]\n### `intercomBridge\n[...]\n`pi-subagents` works standalone through natural language, the `subagent` tool, slash commands, and the packaged prompt shortcuts listed near the top of this README. If you use pi-prompt-template-model, you can also wrap subagent delegation in your own reusable prompt templates.\n[...]\n|------|---------|\n|\n[...]\nsrc/extension/index.\n[...]\n` | Extension registration, tool registration, message/render wiring. |\n| `src/agents/agents.ts` | Agent and chain discovery, frontmatter parsing. |\n| `src/runs/foreground/subagent-executor.ts` | Main execution routing for single, parallel, chain, management, status, interrupt, and doctor actions. |\n| `src/runs/foreground/execution.ts` | Core foreground `runSync` handling. |\n| `src/\n[...]\n/background/sub\n[...]\n` | Detached async\n[...]\n. |\n| `src/runs/background/async-execution.\n[...]\n` | Background\n[...]\n| `src/\n[...]\n/background/async-status.ts` | Status discovery and formatting","error":null}]},"id":"c6e29d75","parentId":"ba544f20","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:59:56.691Z"}
