orks/pi (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md)\n\n```md > pi can help you create pi packages. Ask it to bundle your extensions, skills, prompt templates, or themes. [...] Pi packages bundle extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes so you can share them through npm or git. A package can declare resources in `package.json` under the `pi` key, or use conventional directories. [...] > **Security:** Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions execute arbitrary code, and skills can instruct the model to perform any action including ru\nSource: packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md at main · earendil-works/pi (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md)\n\nMy personal pi configuration — agents, skills, extensions, and prompts that shape how pi works for me. [...] Clone this repo directly to `~/.pi/agent/` — pi auto-discovers everything from there (extensions, skills, agents, AGENTS.md, mcp.json). No symlinks, no manual wiring. [...] 1. Creates `settings.json` with the right packages (if it doesn't exist) 2. Installs all git packages via `pi install` (see Packages below) 3. Runs `npm install` for extensions with dependencies (claude-tool) [...] Loa\nSource: pijusz/pi-config (https://github.com/pijusz/pi-config)\n\nCustom hooks for pi coding agent [...] Custom extensions and skills for pi coding agent. [...] **Requires pi 0.50.0+** | npm | GitHub [...] - branch-sessions.ts - Git branch-based session directories - clipboard.ts - Copy text to system clipboard - oracle.ts - Get second opinions from other AI models - memory-mode.ts - Save instructions to AGENTS.md - plan-mode.ts - Read-only exploration mode - handoff.ts - Transfer context to new sessions - usage-bar.ts - AI provider usage statistics - ultrathi\nSource: hjanuschka/shitty-extensions (https://github.com/hjanuschka/shitty-extensions)\n\nI have been running OpenCode and Hermes Agent for a while now, but Pi kept coming up in conversations. People on Reddit and Hacker News kept calling it \"the minimal one that does not get in your way.\" After two weeks of daily use, I see why. Pi is a terminal coding agent built by Mario Zechner that stays small at the core. You install it, point it at a project, and start working. Everything else — memory, MCP support, sub-agents, themes, skills — gets added through extensions you actually choose\nSource: Pi Coding Agent Setup Guide: Install, Configure Models, and Best Extensions (https://www.bitdoze.com/pi-coding-agent-setup-guide/)\n\nMy personal pi coding agent configuration - skills and extensions [...] My personal pi configuration — agents, skills, extensions, and prompts that shape how pi works for me. [...] Clone this repo directly to `~/.pi/agent/` — pi auto-discovers everything from there (extensions, skills, agents, AGENTS.md, mcp.json). No symlinks, no manual wiring. [...] (https:// [...] .com/ [...] # 2. Clone this repo as your [...] installs packages + [...] This config uses **subagents** — visible pi sessions spaw\nSource: HazAT/pi-config (https://github.com/HazAT/pi-config)\n\nPi is a minimal terminal coding harness. Adapt pi to your workflows, not the other way around, without having to fork and modify pi internals. Extend it with TypeScript Extensions, Skills, Prompt Templates, and Themes. Put your extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes in Pi Packages and share them with others via npm or git. [...] skips features like [...] pi to build [...] Then just talk to pi. By default, pi gives the model four tools: `read`, `write`, `edit`, and `bash`. The model use\nSource: heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md at refs · badlogic/pi-mono (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/refs/heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md)\n\nVS Code-style command palette for selecting and applying skills in pi [...] A command palette for Pi coding agent that lets you explicitly select which skill to inject with your next message. [...] Agents don't always know when to read their skills. Instead of relying on automatic detection based on task context, this extension gives you direct control. Select a skill from the palette and it gets sent alongside your next message. [...] ```bash pi install npm:pi-skill-palette ``` [...] Restart pi\nSource: nicobailon/pi-skill-palette (https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-skill-palette)\n\nPi is a minimal terminal coding harness. A coding agent designed for customization and control, not dictating workflows. [...] ```bash npm install -g @mariozechner/ [...] -coding-agent [...] Pi is aggressively extensible so it doesn't have to dictate your workflow. Features that other tools bake in can be built with extensions, skills, or installed from third-party pi packages. [...] - No MCP (build CLI tools with READMEs or add via extension) - No sub-agents (build your own or install package) \nSource: pi - Coding Agent (https://ailearnedtoday.com/ref/pi.dev)","results":[{"title":"Pi Coding Agent","url":"https://pi.dev/","snippet":""},{"title":"packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md at main · earendil-works/pi","url":"https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md","snippet":""},{"title":"packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md at main · earendil-works/pi","url":"https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md","snippet":""},{"title":"pijusz/pi-config","url":"https://github.com/pijusz/pi-config","snippet":""},{"title":"hjanuschka/shitty-extensions","url":"https://github.com/hjanuschka/shitty-extensions","snippet":""},{"title":"Pi Coding Agent Setup Guide: Install, Configure Models, and Best Extensions","url":"https://www.bitdoze.com/pi-coding-agent-setup-guide/","snippet":""},{"title":"HazAT/pi-config","url":"https://github.com/HazAT/pi-config","snippet":""},{"title":"heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md at refs · badlogic/pi-mono","url":"https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/refs/heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md","snippet":""},{"title":"nicobailon/pi-skill-palette","url":"https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-skill-palette","snippet":""},{"title":"pi - Coding Agent","url":"https://ailearnedtoday.com/ref/pi.dev","snippet":""}],"error":null,"provider":"exa"},{"query":"\"LazyPi\" \"pi-depo\" \"pi install\" package manager comparison","answer":"Declarative package manager for Pi Coding Agent - skills, extensions, hooks, MCP servers [...] Keywords pi-package, pi-coding-agent, package-manager License MIT Install` npm install pi-depo@0.1.44 ` [...] # pi-depo (pd) [...] Declarative package manager for Pi Coding Agent. Manage skills, extensions, and MCP servers from a single`kit.yml`, synced across machines via GitHub Gist. [...] ``` bun i -g pi-depo [...] That's it.`pd login` finds your existing gist, pulls`kit.yml`, and installs everythin\nSource: pi-depo 0.1.44 on npm - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software (https://libraries.io/npm/pi-depo)\n\nInstallation — LazyPi Docs [...] Get LazyPi running in under a minute. [...] - Node.js 18 or later - npm (bundled with Node.js) - Optional: Bun, if you want the official Compound Engineering package from Every [...] You do not need to install LazyPi globally.`npx` downloads and runs it on demand. [...] If Bun is missing, LazyPi still installs everything else. It simply warns that Compound Engineering will be skipped. [...] ## Run it [...] ``` npx @robzolkos/lazypi ``` [...] That's the entire ins\nSource: Installation — LazyPi Docs (https://lazypi.org/docs/installation.html)\n\nLazyPi — One command. Full Pi setup. [...] Pi coding agent · instant setup [...] One command. Everything configured. Nothing to research. [...] One command installs 60+ community skills, 67 themes, MCP support, sub-agent support, Claude Code CLI provider support, memory, and more. You're left with a curated starting point that gets you an exciting, useful experience immediately, without the research and configuration tax. [...] ❯ npx @robzolkos/lazypi [...] ◆ Install everything or pick packages?\nSource: LazyPi — One command. Full Pi setup. (https://lazypi.org/)\n\nOne command to get a complete, curated Pi coding agent setup. [...] Pi is a minimalist coding agent. That minimalism is intentional — it ships as a blank canvas and lets the community build on top of it. The result is a rich ecosystem of packages: skills, themes, MCP adapters, memory systems, prompt templates, sub-agents, cost trackers, and engineering frameworks. [...] LazyPi is the fastest path into that ecosystem. Run one command and you get a curated selection of the community's best work, p\nSource: Overview — LazyPi Docs (https://lazypi.org/docs/)\n\n# Repository: robzolkos/LazyPi Pi coding agent setup for the lazy - Stars: 88 - Forks: 3 - Watchers: 0 - Open issues: 2 - Primary language: JavaScript - Languages: JavaScript - License: MIT License (MIT) - Default branch: master - Homepage: https://lazypi.org - Created: 2026-04-18T16:02:41Z - Last push: 2026-04-23T01:11:03Z - Contributors: 2 (top: robzolkos, github-actions[bot]) - Releases: 7 - Latest release: v0.2.0 (2026-04-23T00:20:41Z) --- # LazyPi The Pi coding agent is minimal by design. L\nSource: robzolkos/LazyPi (https://github.com/robzolkos/LazyPi)\n\nKeep your LazyPi packages current. [...] ## Update all packages [...] ``` npx @robzolkos/lazypi update ``` [...] This reconciles the LazyPi catalog against what's currently installed and runs`pi update` for each package that has a newer version available. [...] LazyPi maintains a catalog of the exact packages and versions it manages. When you run`update`, it: [...] 1. Compares the current catalog against your installed packages 2. Identifies packages with newer versions 3. Runs`pi update` for ea\nSource: Updating — LazyPi Docs (https://lazypi.org/docs/updating.html)\n\nBelow you will find installation steps, model configuration with cheap providers, the extensions I actually use, and how to skip the research phase with LazyPi. [...] LazyPi: one-command setup with 60+ skills, 76 themes, and MCP support [...] ## Installing Pi [...] Pi is distributed as an npm package. [...] ```bash npm install -g @mariozechner/pi-coding-agent ``` [...] Pi needs Node.js 18+. If you do not have Node, install it first: [...] ```bash curl -o- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvm-sh\nSource: Pi Coding Agent Setup Guide: Install, Configure Models, and Best Extensions (https://www.bitdoze.com/pi-coding-agent-setup-guide/)\n\nFirst Steps — [...] The steps below assume you chose Install all. If you installed a smaller subset, just use the parts that match the packages you selected. [...] ## 1. Launch Pi [...] LazyPi installs and configures packages, but Pi is still the app you use day to day. Open it in a real project and you should immediately see the extra commands, tools, themes, and UI enhancements LazyPi added. [...] Installing LazyPi does not automatically sign you into a model provider. The easiest path is to a\nSource: First Steps — LazyPi Docs (https://lazypi.org/docs/first-steps.html)\n\nPi is powerful. But like Neovim, Arch Linux, or tmux — its minimalism is also its barrier. Out of the box, there's not much to see. The magic lives in the ecosystem: the skills, the themes, the tools the community has built around it. LazyPi is to Pi what LazyVim is to Neovim, or Omarchy is to Arch — a curated starting point that gets you to an exciting, useful experience immediately, without the research and configuration tax. [...] Do I need to install [...] No. If Pi isn't installed on your m\nSource: FAQ — LazyPi (https://lazypi.org/faq.html)\n\nI released LazyPi and some Pi [...] One of the main reasons people like Pi is that it starts small. It does not try to bundle every convenience by default. It gives you primitives, a clear extension model, and room to build your own workflow. For a lot of people, that is the whole point. I get that because it is exactly what drew me in too, and why Pi is now my daily driver coding agent. [...] LazyPi goes against that. [...] It adds a curated set of extensions, skills, and themes up front so tha\nSource: LazyPi made people mad (https://www.zolkos.com/2026/04/21/lazypi-made-people-mad)","results":[{"title":"pi-depo 0.1.44 on npm - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software","url":"https://libraries.io/npm/pi-depo","snippet":""},{"title":"Installation — LazyPi Docs","url":"https://lazypi.org/docs/installation.html","snippet":""},{"title":"LazyPi — One command. Full Pi setup.","url":"https://lazypi.org/","snippet":""},{"title":"Overview — LazyPi Docs","url":"https://lazypi.org/docs/","snippet":""},{"title":"robzolkos/LazyPi","url":"https://github.com/robzolkos/LazyPi","snippet":""},{"title":"Updating — LazyPi Docs","url":"https://lazypi.org/docs/updating.html","snippet":""},{"title":"Pi Coding Agent Setup Guide: Install, Configure Models, and Best Extensions","url":"https://www.bitdoze.com/pi-coding-agent-setup-guide/","snippet":""},{"title":"First Steps — LazyPi Docs","url":"https://lazypi.org/docs/first-steps.html","snippet":""},{"title":"FAQ — LazyPi","url":"https://lazypi.org/faq.html","snippet":""},{"title":"LazyPi made people mad","url":"https://www.zolkos.com/2026/04/21/lazypi-made-people-mad","snippet":""}],"error":null,"provider":"exa"}]},"id":"9df789e5","parentId":"dbf8fac4","timestamp":"2026-05-16T13:01:57.156Z"}
{"type":"message","id":"5f33d222","parentId":"9df789e5","timestamp":"2026-05-16T13:01:57.156Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_KNVwlABKRvlKeSbnUmzUGAAW|fc_02f887b473d5faa9016a086abe90648190be3b45266b4ea9ff","toolName":"web_search","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Query: \"\"declarative package manager\" \"Pi Coding Agent\" \"kit.yml\" \"pi-depo\" alternatives\"\n\nDeclarative dependency manager for AI coding agent skills and subagents [...] Declarative dependency manager for AI coding agent skills and subagents. [...] Manage skills and subagent definitions from git repositories across multiple coding agents (Pi, Claude Code, Cursor, OpenCode, Codex, Amp, and more) with a single `agents.yaml` file. [...] - **Clone method**: SSH or HTTPS - **Target agents**: Which coding agents you use (Pi, Claude Code, Cursor, etc.) - **Install method**: Link (symlinks, de\nSource: jgordijn/agentdeps (https://github.com/jgordijn/agentdeps)\n\nCanonical configuration manager for AI coding agent components [...] **agentmux** is a CLI tool that manages AI coding agent configurations — agents, commands, skills, rules, instructions, and MCP servers — from a single canonical format (Markdown + YAML frontmatter) and syncs them across all your AI tools at once. [...] Write your agent configurations once. Deploy everywhere. [...] ## Supported Tools [...] | Tool | Agents | Commands | Skills | Rules | Instructions | MCP | |------|:------:|:----\nSource: agentmux v1.0.2 (https://pypi.org/project/agentmux/)\n\nAI agents as code. Declarative LLM orchestration in YAML. Compose multi-step workflows across models, APIs, and data sources with fully defined control flow. [...] # kdeps: AI agents as code. [...] Build autonomous AI agents in YAML — wire LLMs, APIs, databases, and Python scripts with no glue code. [...] . Do not use [...] ```bash kdeps new # scaffolds a project interactively kdeps run workflow.yaml --dev # hot-reload, no Docker needed ``` [...] ## Key concepts [...] | Concept | What it does | \nSource: kdeps/kdeps (https://github.com/kdeps/kdeps/)\n\nI set up a shared Git repo for my team to maintain reusable AI agent packages, skills, instructions, coding standards, custom agents, and APM (Agent Package Manager), a tool by Microsoft that distributes these packages to every project in one command. APM manages agent configuration across repos and is tool-agnostic and can handle transitive dependencies, lockfiles, and deploying to multiple tools at once. However, this method of managing agent configuration was criticized for creating a false s\nSource: How I Set Up Shared Agent Config for Our Team with APM (https://dev.to/dantederuwe/how-i-set-up-shared-agent-config-for-our-team-with-apm-3ncb)\n\nDeclarative package manager for Pi Coding Agent - skills, extensions, hooks, MCP servers [...] Declarative package manager for Pi Coding Agent. Manage skills, extensions, and MCP servers from a single `kit.yml`, synced across machines via GitHub Gist. [...] That's it. `pd login` finds your existing gist, pulls `kit.yml`, and installs everything. [...] ```bash pd login # authenticate pd init # scan current pi installation → generate kit.yml # OR import from a friend's public gist pd push # save t\nSource: pi-depo (https://registry.npmjs.org/pi-depo)\n\n```md > pi can help you create pi packages. Ask it to bundle your extensions, skills, prompt templates, or themes. # Pi Packages [...] Pi packages bundle extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes so you can share them through npm or git. A package can declare resources in `package.json` under the `pi` key, or use conventional directories. [...] ## Creating a Pi Package [...] Add a `pi` manifest to `package.json` or use conventional directories. Include the `pi-package` keyword for discove\nSource: packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md at main · earendil-works/pi (https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md)\n\n# kdeps: AI agents as code. [...] Build autonomous AI agents in YAML — wire LLMs, APIs, databases, and Python scripts with no glue code. [...] > **Highly experimental [...] , and CLI [...] . Do not use in [...] . Report issues or give feedback. [...] ```bash kdeps new [...] a project interact [...] kdeps run workflow.yaml --dev # hot-reload, no Docker needed [...] ? Install a component: [...] Components are self-contained capability packages. Install globally, call from anywhere. [...] ```bash k\nSource: github.com/kdeps/kdeps v0.6.13 (https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/kdeps/kdeps)\n\nPi is a minimal terminal coding harness. Adapt Pi to your workflows, not the other way around. Customize Pi with extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes. Bundle them as Pi packages and share via npm or git. [...] Pi ships with powerful defaults but skips features like sub-agents and plan mode. Ask Pi to build what you want, or install a package that does it your way. [...] Pi isn't a sealed product. If you need a command, tool, provider, workflow, or UI tweak, just ask Pi to build it. I\nSource: Pi Coding Agent (https://pi.dev/)\n\nPi is a minimal terminal coding harness. Adapt pi to your workflows, not the other way around, without having to fork and modify pi internals. Extend it with TypeScript Extensions, Skills, Prompt Templates, and Themes. Put your extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes in Pi Packages and share them with others via npm or git. [...] Pi ships with powerful defaults but skips features like sub agents and plan mode. Instead, you can ask pi to build what you want or install a third party pi pa\nSource: packages/coding-agent/README.md at main · earendil-works/pi (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md)\n\napt-bundle — declarative apt package management ▸ brew bundle — but for apt # Declarative packagesfor Debian & Ubuntu. Define your system dependencies once in an`Aptfile`. Install, sync, and audit them with a single command — idempotently, every time. Install `$ curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apt-bundle/apt-bundle/main/install.sh | sudo bash` Copy Run `$ sudo apt-bundle` Copy Other install methods → Releases Aptfile reference ## See it in action One Aptfile. One command. Your packa\nSource: apt-bundle — declarative apt package management (https://apt-bundle.org/)\n\n---\n\n**Sources:**\n1. jgordijn/agentdeps\n   https://github.com/jgordijn/agentdeps\n\n2. agentmux v1.0.2\n   https://pypi.org/project/agentmux/\n\n3. kdeps/kdeps\n   https://github.com/kdeps/kdeps/\n\n4. How I Set Up Shared Agent Config for Our Team with APM\n   https://dev.to/dantederuwe/how-i-set-up-shared-agent-config-for-our-team-with-apm-3ncb\n\n5. pi-depo\n   https://registry.npmjs.org/pi-depo\n\n6. packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md at main · earendil-works/pi\n   https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md\n\n7. github.com/kdeps/kdeps v0.6.13\n   https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/kdeps/kdeps\n\n8. Pi Coding Agent\n   https://pi.dev/\n\n9. packages/coding-agent/README.md at main · earendil-works/pi\n   https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md\n\n10. apt-bundle — declarative apt package management\n   https://apt-bundle.org/\n\n## Query: \"\"pi-depo\" \"profiles\" \"Pi Coding Agent\" \"pd diff\"\"\n\nDeclarative package manager for Pi Coding Agent - skills, extensions, hooks, MCP servers [...] # pi-depo (`pd`) [...] Declarative package manager for Pi Coding Agent. Manage skills, extensions, and MCP servers from a single `kit.yml`, synced across machines via GitHub Gist. [...] from someone's public gist?\" → y # → \"GitHub username or username/profile:\" [...] fulgidus # → [...] their kit.yml, offers to [...] 1. **Self-update** - checks npm for a newer `pi-depo`, installs and restarts if found 2\nSource: pi-depo (https://registry.npmjs.org/pi-depo)\n\nDeclarative package manager for Pi Coding Agent - skills, extensions, hooks, MCP servers [...] # pi-depo (pd) [...] Declarative package manager for Pi Coding Agent. Manage skills, extensions, and MCP servers from a single`kit.yml`, synced across machines via GitHub Gist. [...] ``` pd login pd init # → \"Bootstrap from someone's public gist?\" → y # → \"GitHub username or username/profile:\" → fulgidus # → imports their kit.yml, offers to sync immediately ``` [...] pd login [...] authenticate pd init\nSource: pi-depo 0.1.44 on npm - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software (https://libraries.io/npm/pi-depo)\n\n: - [...] +```bash +pd # sync everything (default command) +pd sync # same -| Command | Description | -|---------|-------------| -| `pkit init` | Bootstrap `kit.yml` from current Pi installation | -| `pkit sync` | Desired state → real state. Idempotent. | -| `pkit status` | Show package status (synced/missing/disabled/orphan) | -| `pkit diff` | Dry-run sync - show what would change | -| `pkit verify` | Run verify checks for all packages | -| `pkit upgrade` | Update non-pinned packages to latest \nSource: feat: pd init imports from public gist + full README rewrite · fb0e506 · fulgidus/pi-depo (https://github.com/fulgidus/pi-depo/commit/fb0e506d9ee148219367b7282648c7115582583e)\n\nPi Coding Agent [...] Pi is a minimal terminal coding harness. Adapt Pi to your workflows, not the other way around. Customize Pi with extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes. Bundle them as Pi packages and share via npm or git. [...] Pi ships with powerful defaults but skips features like sub-agents and plan mode. Ask Pi to build what you want, or install a package that does it your way. [...] Pi isn't a sealed product. If you need a command, tool, provider, workflow, or UI tweak, just\nSource: Pi Coding Agent (https://pi.dev/)\n\nPi Coding Agent [...] Pi is a minimal terminal coding harness. Adapt Pi to your workflows, not the other way around. Customize Pi with extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes. Bundle them as Pi packages and share via npm or git. [...] Pi ships with powerful defaults but skips features like sub-agents and plan mode. Ask Pi to build what you want, or install a package that does it your way. [...] Pi isn't a sealed product. If you need a command, tool, provider, workflow, or UI tweak, just\nSource: Pi Coding Agent (https://shittycodingagent.ai/)\n\nCoding agent CLI with read, bash, edit, write tools and session management forked from @mariozechner/pi-coding-agent [...] > _Current focus: at [...] moment i'm deep in refactoring internals, and need to focus._ --- pi.dev domain graciously donated by exe.dev Pi is a minimal terminal coding harness. Adapt pi to your workflows, not the other way around, without having to fork and modify pi internals. Extend it with TypeScript Extensions, Skills, Prompt Templates, and Themes. Put your extensions, \nSource: @vandeepunk/pi-coding-agent (https://registry.npmjs.org/@vandeepunk/pi-coding-agent)\n\nI have been running OpenCode and Hermes Agent for a while now, but Pi kept coming up in conversations. People on Reddit and Hacker News kept calling it \"the minimal one that does not get in your way.\" After two weeks of daily use, I see why. Pi is a terminal coding agent built by Mario Zechner that stays small at the core. You install it, point it at a project, and start working. Everything else — memory, MCP support, sub-agents, themes, skills — gets added through extensions you actually choose\nSource: Pi Coding Agent Setup Guide: Install, Configure Models, and Best Extensions (https://www.bitdoze.com/pi-coding-agent-setup-guide/)\n\n# Repository: capdevc/pi-codex-profile (fork of Graffioh/pi-codex-profile) [...] # pi-codex-profile [...] Codex performance profile for pi coding agent. No command needed: once the extension is loaded (`/reload`), it works automatically when your active model is Codex. [...] ### 1) Codex operating profile (auto-injected) [...] When a Codex model is active, the extension appends a comprehensive Codex profile to the system prompt. It includes high-impact coding guidance adapted from the Codex Prom\nSource: capdevc/pi-codex-profile (https://github.com/capdevc/pi-codex-profile)\n\n# Repository: zhouzhuojie/pi-agents (fork of michalvavra/agents) [...] Configuration for pi AI coding agents. [...] # Agent Configuration [...] Configuration for AI coding agents. [...] ## [pi][pi-coding-agent] extensions [...] - `skill-dollar.ts`: suggests `$skill-name` completions in the editor and injects skill usage guidance when mentioned. Idea from Codex. [...] ```bash ln -sf {baseDir}/AGENTS.md ~/.pi/agent/AGENTS.md ln -sf {baseDir}/skills ~/.pi/agent/skills ln -sf {baseDir}/agents/pi/pro\nSource: zhouzhuojie/pi-agents (https://github.com/zhouzhuojie/pi-agents)\n\n* pi-coding-agent: Coding Agent CLI [...] # pi-coding-agent: Coding Agent CLI [...] The `@mariozechner/pi-coding-agent` package provides an interactive terminal-based coding assistant with tool execution, session persistence, and extensibility. This page documents the overall architecture, core abstractions, and how the system components interact. It serves as the architectural overview for the coding-agent package. [...] The coding agent is built around `AgentSession`, which orchestrates the ag\nSource: pi-coding-agent: Coding Agent CLI | badlogic/pi-mono | DeepWiki (https://deepwiki.com/badlogic/pi-mono/4-pi-coding-agent:-coding-agent-cli)\n\n---\n\n**Sources:**\n1. pi-depo\n   https://registry.npmjs.org/pi-depo\n\n2. pi-depo 0.1.44 on npm - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software\n   https://libraries.io/npm/pi-depo\n\n3. feat: pd init imports from public gist + full README rewrite · fb0e506 · fulgidus/pi-depo\n   https://github.com/fulgidus/pi-depo/commit/fb0e506d9ee148219367b7282648c7115582583e\n\n4. Pi Coding Agent\n   https://pi.dev/\n\n5. Pi Coding Agent\n   https://shittycodingagent.ai/\n\n6. @vandeepunk/pi-coding-agent\n   https://registry.npmjs.org/@vandeepunk/pi-coding-agent\n\n7. Pi Coding Agent Setup Guide: Install, Configure Models, and Best Extensions\n   https://www.bitdoze.com/pi-coding-agent-setup-guide/\n\n8. capdevc/pi-codex-profile\n   https://github.com/capdevc/pi-codex-profile\n\n9. zhouzhuojie/pi-agents\n   https://github.com/zhouzhuojie/pi-agents\n\n10. pi-coding-agent: Coding Agent CLI | badlogic/pi-mono | DeepWiki\n   https://deepwiki.com/badlogic/pi-mono/4-pi-coding-agent:-coding-agent-cli\n\n## Query: \"\"Pi Coding Agent\" \"flow profiles\" extensions skills install on demand\"\n\nPi is a minimal terminal coding harness. Adapt Pi to your workflows, not the other way around. Customize Pi with extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes. Bundle them as Pi packages and share via npm or git. [...] Pi ships with powerful defaults but skips features like sub-agents and plan mode. Ask Pi to build what you want, or install a package that does it your way. [...] Pi isn't a sealed product. If you need a command, tool, provider, workflow, or UI tweak, just ask Pi to build it. I\nSource: Pi Coding Agent (https://pi.dev/)\n\nExtensions are TypeScript modules that extend pi's behavior. They can subscribe to lifecycle events, register custom tools callable by the LLM, add commands, and more. [...] > **Placement for /reload:** Put extensions in `~/.pi/agent/extensions/` (global) or `.pi/extensions/` (project-local) for auto-discovery. Use `pi -e ./path.ts` only for quick tests. Extensions in auto-discovered locations can be hot-reloaded with `/reload`. [...] tools** - [...] or modify tool [...] context, customize [...]\nSource: packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md at main · earendil-works/pi (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md)\n\n```md > pi can help you create pi packages. Ask it to bundle your extensions, skills, prompt templates, or themes. [...] Pi packages bundle extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes so you can share them through npm or git. A package can declare resources in `package.json` under the `pi` key, or use conventional directories. [...] > **Security:** Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions execute arbitrary code, and skills can instruct the model to perform any action including ru\nSource: packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md at main · earendil-works/pi (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md)\n\nMy personal pi configuration — agents, skills, extensions, and prompts that shape how pi works for me. [...] Clone this repo directly to `~/.pi/agent/` — pi auto-discovers everything from there (extensions, skills, agents, AGENTS.md, mcp.json). No symlinks, no manual wiring. [...] 1. Creates `settings.json` with the right packages (if it doesn't exist) 2. Installs all git packages via `pi install` (see Packages below) 3. Runs `npm install` for extensions with dependencies (claude-tool) [...] Loa\nSource: pijusz/pi-config (https://github.com/pijusz/pi-config)\n\nCustom hooks for pi coding agent [...] Custom extensions and skills for pi coding agent. [...] **Requires pi 0.50.0+** | npm | GitHub [...] - branch-sessions.ts - Git branch-based session directories - clipboard.ts - Copy text to system clipboard - oracle.ts - Get second opinions from other AI models - memory-mode.ts - Save instructions to AGENTS.md - plan-mode.ts - Read-only exploration mode - handoff.ts - Transfer context to new sessions - usage-bar.ts - AI provider usage statistics - ultrathi\nSource: hjanuschka/shitty-extensions (https://github.com/hjanuschka/shitty-extensions)\n\nI have been running OpenCode and Hermes Agent for a while now, but Pi kept coming up in conversations. People on Reddit and Hacker News kept calling it \"the minimal one that does not get in your way.\" After two weeks of daily use, I see why. Pi is a terminal coding agent built by Mario Zechner that stays small at the core. You install it, point it at a project, and start working. Everything else — memory, MCP support, sub-agents, themes, skills — gets added through extensions you actually choose\nSource: Pi Coding Agent Setup Guide: Install, Configure Models, and Best Extensions (https://www.bitdoze.com/pi-coding-agent-setup-guide/)\n\nMy personal pi coding agent configuration - skills and extensions [...] My personal pi configuration — agents, skills, extensions, and prompts that shape how pi works for me. [...] Clone this repo directly to `~/.pi/agent/` — pi auto-discovers everything from there (extensions, skills, agents, AGENTS.md, mcp.json). No symlinks, no manual wiring. [...] (https:// [...] .com/ [...] # 2. Clone this repo as your [...] installs packages + [...] This config uses **subagents** — visible pi sessions spaw\nSource: HazAT/pi-config (https://github.com/HazAT/pi-config)\n\nPi is a minimal terminal coding harness. Adapt pi to your workflows, not the other way around, without having to fork and modify pi internals. Extend it with TypeScript Extensions, Skills, Prompt Templates, and Themes. Put your extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes in Pi Packages and share them with others via npm or git. [...] skips features like [...] pi to build [...] Then just talk to pi. By default, pi gives the model four tools: `read`, `write`, `edit`, and `bash`. The model use\nSource: heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md at refs · badlogic/pi-mono (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/refs/heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md)\n\nVS Code-style command palette for selecting and applying skills in pi [...] A command palette for Pi coding agent that lets you explicitly select which skill to inject with your next message. [...] Agents don't always know when to read their skills. Instead of relying on automatic detection based on task context, this extension gives you direct control. Select a skill from the palette and it gets sent alongside your next message. [...] ```bash pi install npm:pi-skill-palette ``` [...] Restart pi\nSource: nicobailon/pi-skill-palette (https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-skill-palette)\n\nPi is a minimal terminal coding harness. A coding agent designed for customization and control, not dictating workflows. [...] ```bash npm install -g @mariozechner/ [...] -coding-agent [...] Pi is aggressively extensible so it doesn't have to dictate your workflow. Features that other tools bake in can be built with extensions, skills, or installed from third-party pi packages. [...] - No MCP (build CLI tools with READMEs or add via extension) - No sub-agents (build your own or install package) \nSource: pi - Coding Agent (https://ailearnedtoday.com/ref/pi.dev)\n\n---\n\n**Sources:**\n1. Pi Coding Agent\n   https://pi.dev/\n\n2. packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md at main · earendil-works/pi\n   https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md\n\n3. packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md at main · earendil-works/pi\n   https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md\n\n4. pijusz/pi-config\n   https://github.com/pijusz/pi-config\n\n5. hjanuschka/shitty-extensions\n   https://github.com/hjanuschka/shitty-extensions\n\n6. Pi Coding Agent Setup Guide: Install, Configure Models, and Best Extensions\n   https://www.bitdoze.com/pi-coding-agent-setup-guide/\n\n7. HazAT/pi-config\n   https://github.com/HazAT/pi-config\n\n8. heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md at refs · badlogic/pi-mono\n   https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/refs/heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md\n\n9. nicobailon/pi-skill-palette\n   https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-skill-palette\n\n10. pi - Coding Agent\n   https://ailearnedtoday.com/ref/pi.dev\n\n## Query: \"\"LazyPi\" \"pi-depo\" \"pi install\" package manager comparison\"\n\nDeclarative package manager for Pi Coding Agent - skills, extensions, hooks, MCP servers [...] Keywords pi-package, pi-coding-agent, package-manager License MIT Install` npm install pi-depo@0.1.44 ` [...] # pi-depo (pd) [...] Declarative package manager for Pi Coding Agent. Manage skills, extensions, and MCP servers from a single`kit.yml`, synced across machines via GitHub Gist. [...] ``` bun i -g pi-depo [...] That's it.`pd login` finds your existing gist, pulls`kit.yml`, and installs everythin\nSource: pi-depo 0.1.44 on npm - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software (https://libraries.io/npm/pi-depo)\n\nInstallation — LazyPi Docs [...] Get LazyPi running in under a minute. [...] - Node.js 18 or later - npm (bundled with Node.js) - Optional: Bun, if you want the official Compound Engineering package from Every [...] You do not need to install LazyPi globally.`npx` downloads and runs it on demand. [...] If Bun is missing, LazyPi still installs everything else. It simply warns that Compound Engineering will be skipped. [...] ## Run it [...] ``` npx @robzolkos/lazypi ``` [...] That's the entire ins\nSource: Installation — LazyPi Docs (https://lazypi.org/docs/installation.html)\n\nLazyPi — One command. Full Pi setup. [...] Pi coding agent · instant setup [...] One command. Everything configured. Nothing to research. [...] One command installs 60+ community skills, 67 themes, MCP support, sub-agent support, Claude Code CLI provider support, memory, and more. You're left with a curated starting point that gets you an exciting, useful experience immediately, without the research and configuration tax. [...] ❯ npx @robzolkos/lazypi [...] ◆ Install everything or pick packages?\nSource: LazyPi — One command. Full Pi setup. (https://lazypi.org/)\n\nOne command to get a complete, curated Pi coding agent setup. [...] Pi is a minimalist coding agent. That minimalism is intentional — it ships as a blank canvas and lets the community build on top of it. The result is a rich ecosystem of packages: skills, themes, MCP adapters, memory systems, prompt templates, sub-agents, cost trackers, and engineering frameworks. [...] LazyPi is the fastest path into that ecosystem. Run one command and you get a curated selection of the community's best work, p\nSource: Overview — LazyPi Docs (https://lazypi.org/docs/)\n\n# Repository: robzolkos/LazyPi Pi coding agent setup for the lazy - Stars: 88 - Forks: 3 - Watchers: 0 - Open issues: 2 - Primary language: JavaScript - Languages: JavaScript - License: MIT License (MIT) - Default branch: master - Homepage: https://lazypi.org - Created: 2026-04-18T16:02:41Z - Last push: 2026-04-23T01:11:03Z - Contributors: 2 (top: robzolkos, github-actions[bot]) - Releases: 7 - Latest release: v0.2.0 (2026-04-23T00:20:41Z) --- # LazyPi The Pi coding agent is minimal by design. L\nSource: robzolkos/LazyPi (https://github.com/robzolkos/LazyPi)\n\nKeep your LazyPi packages current. [...] ## Update all packages [...] ``` npx @robzolkos/lazypi update ``` [...] This reconciles the LazyPi catalog against what's currently installed and runs`pi update` for each package that has a newer version available. [...] LazyPi maintains a catalog of the exact packages and versions it manages. When you run`update`, it: [...] 1. Compares the current catalog against your installed packages 2. Identifies packages with newer versions 3. Runs`pi update` for ea\nSource: Updating — LazyPi Docs (https://lazypi.org/docs/updating.html)\n\nBelow you will find installation steps, model configuration with cheap providers, the extensions I actually use, and how to skip the research phase with LazyPi. [...] LazyPi: one-command setup with 60+ skills, 76 themes, and MCP support [...] ## Installing Pi [...] Pi is distributed as an npm package. [...] ```bash npm install -g @mariozechner/pi-coding-agent ``` [...] Pi needs Node.js 18+. If you do not have Node, install it first: [...] ```bash curl -o- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvm-sh\nSource: Pi Coding Agent Setup Guide: Install, Configure Models, and Best Extensions (https://www.bitdoze.com/pi-coding-agent-setup-guide/)\n\nFirst Steps — [...] The steps below assume you chose Install all. If you installed a smaller subset, just use the parts that match the packages you selected. [...] ## 1. Launch Pi [...] LazyPi installs and configures packages, but Pi is still the app you use day to day. Open it in a real project and you should immediately see the extra commands, tools, themes, and UI enhancements LazyPi added. [...] Installing LazyPi does not automatically sign you into a model provider. The easiest path is to a\nSource: First Steps — LazyPi Docs (https://lazypi.org/docs/first-steps.html)\n\nPi is powerful. But like Neovim, Arch Linux, or tmux — its minimalism is also its barrier. Out of the box, there's not much to see. The magic lives in the ecosystem: the skills, the themes, the tools the community has built around it. LazyPi is to Pi what LazyVim is to Neovim, or Omarchy is to Arch — a curated starting point that gets you to an exciting, useful experience immediately, without the research and configuration tax. [...] Do I need to install [...] No. If Pi isn't installed on your m\nSource: FAQ — LazyPi (https://lazypi.org/faq.html)\n\nI released LazyPi and some Pi [...] One of the main reasons people like Pi is that it starts small. It does not try to bundle every convenience by default. It gives you primitives, a clear extension model, and room to build your own workflow. For a lot of people, that is the whole point. I get that because it is exactly what drew me in too, and why Pi is now my daily driver coding agent. [...] LazyPi goes against that. [...] It adds a curated set of extensions, skills, and themes up front so tha\nSource: LazyPi made people mad (https://www.zolkos.com/2026/04/21/lazypi-made-people-mad)\n\n---\n\n**Sources:**\n1. pi-depo 0.1.44 on npm - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software\n   https://libraries.io/npm/pi-depo\n\n2. Installation — LazyPi Docs\n   https://lazypi.org/docs/installation.html\n\n3. LazyPi — One command. Full Pi setup.\n   https://lazypi.org/\n\n4. Overview — LazyPi Docs\n   https://lazypi.org/docs/\n\n5. robzolkos/LazyPi\n   https://github.com/robzolkos/LazyPi\n\n6. Updating — LazyPi Docs\n   https://lazypi.org/docs/updating.html\n\n7. Pi Coding Agent Setup Guide: Install, Configure Models, and Best Extensions\n   https://www.bitdoze.com/pi-coding-agent-setup-guide/\n\n8. First Steps — LazyPi Docs\n   https://lazypi.org/docs/first-steps.html\n\n9. FAQ — LazyPi\n   https://lazypi.org/faq.html\n\n10. LazyPi made people mad\n   https://www.zolkos.com/2026/04/21/lazypi-made-people-mad\n\n---\nFull content for 40 sources available [mp8cvdibsi6b6u]."}],"details":{"queries":["\"declarative package manager\" \"Pi Coding Agent\" \"kit.yml\" \"pi-depo\" alternatives","\"pi-depo\" \"profiles\" \"Pi Coding Agent\" \"pd diff\"","\"Pi Coding Agent\" \"flow profiles\" extensions skills install on demand","\"LazyPi\" \"pi-depo\" \"pi install\" package manager comparison"],"queryCount":4,"successfulQueries":4,"totalResults":40,"includeContent":true,"fetchId":"mp8cvdibsi6b6u","searchId":"mp8cvdic9gt8qd"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1778936517156}}
