{"type":"message","id":"f5196e86","parentId":"bb5d4b05","timestamp":"2026-05-16T13:04:04.561Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_GuWcY6fVvYKNRwMAeZ36KUHT|fc_02f887b473d5faa9016a086b3cc95481908adde098a12e84e3","name":"log_experiment","arguments":{"commit":"ac3a3ba","metric":240,"status":"keep","description":"Validate /atlas-flow command implementation path in Pi docs","metrics":{"sources_reviewed":240},"asi":{"hypothesis":"The /atlas-flow installer should be a tiny Atlas-owned extension command rather than only a prompt template, because it must inspect manifests, prompt/confirm, and run package installation or settings edits.","new_findings":"Pi prompt templates are excellent for domain playbooks and accept argument hints/positional args, but only expand prompts. Extensions can register commands via pi.registerCommand, use ctx.ui for confirm/select, and run logic. Pi package docs confirm pi install defaults to global settings; -l writes project .pi/settings.json and project packages auto-install on startup. Package object filters can load only selected extensions/skills/prompts/themes, which is important for broad packages like Heart of Gold or tmustier bundles. pi-prompt-template-model can add model/skill/thinking behavior for command-like templates, but it still is not an installer.","recommendation_shift":"Implement /atlas-flow as a low-invasiveness global extension in Atlas. Keep domain playbooks as prompt templates/skills. Command UX: list/status/install/remove/diff/doctor; default to project-local installs (-l) for flow packages; require explicit --global for global installs; write/preview manifest-derived package filters; support dry-run first.","overfit_guard":"Chose the mechanism based on Pi capability boundaries, not popularity. Avoided converting everything into an extension: playbook text remains prompts/skills where simpler."}}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"inp