{"type":"message","id":"c58b7306","parentId":"bc24b420","timestamp":"2026-05-16T13:04:28.085Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_sN2LdnAmZ4FArvj6jbLwofqd|fc_02f887b473d5faa9016a086b57450881908785cc5b71b6020e","toolName":"web_search","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Query: \"\"Pi packages run with full system access\" security review packages pi coding agent\"\n\n> **Security:** Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions execute arbitrary code, and skills can instruct the model to perform any action including running executables. Review source code before installing third-party packages.\nSource: packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md at main · earendil-works/pi (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md)\n\n### Pi Packages [...] Bundle and share extensions, skills, prompts, and themes via npm or git. Find packages on npmjs.com or Discord. [...] > **Security:** Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions execute arbitrary code, and skills can instruct the model to perform any action including running executables. Review source code before installing third-party packages. [...] ```bash pi install npm:@foo/pi-tools pi install npm:@foo/pi-tools@1.2.3 # pinned version pi install git:github.com/u\nSource: @mariozechner/pi-coding-agent (https://registry.npmjs.org/@mariozechner/pi-coding-agent)\n\nSecurity: Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions execute arbitrary code, and skills can instruct the model to perform any action including running executables. Review source code before installing third-party packages. [...] dependencies belong in`dependencies` in`package. [...] `. Dependencies that do not register [...] also belong in`dependencies`. When [...] installs a package [...] `npm install\nSource: Pi Packages - Pi Coding Agent (https://pi.dev/docs/latest/packages)\n\nterminal coding agent that powers OpenClaw, built [...] 4 core tools [...] Pi takes an [...] agents bake in [...] - Opinionated minimalism — If you want features out of the box, you'll need to build or install extensions - Extension security — Extensions run with full system access; review third-party packages carefully - No MCP support — Deliberate omission, but requires workarounds for MCP-dependent workflows - Single maintainer — Mario is responsive but it's not a company; bus factor = 1 - Te\nSource: Pi Coding Agent | Ry Walker Research | Ry Walker (https://rywalker.com/research/pi)\n\n### Pi Packages [...] Bundle and share extensions, skills, prompts, and themes via npm or git. Find packages on npmjs.com or Discord. [...] > **Security:** Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions execute arbitrary code, and skills can instruct the model to perform any action including running executables. Review source code before installing third-party packages. [...] ```bash pi install npm:@foo/pi-tools pi install npm:@foo/pi-tools@1.2.3 # pinned version pi install git:github.com/u\nSource: open-pi-coding-agent (https://registry.npmjs.org/open-pi-coding-agent)\n\nPi is distributed as an npm package: [...] ```bash npm install -g @mariozechner/pi-coding-agent [...] in the project directory you [...] Pi can use subscription providers through `/login`, or API-key providers through environment variables or the auth file. [...] Then select a provider. Built-in subscription logins include Claude Pro/Max, ChatGPT Plus/Pro (Codex), and GitHub Copilot. [...] You can also run `/login` and select an API-key provider to store the key in `~/.pi/agent/auth.json`. [...]\nSource: packages/coding-agent/docs/quickstart.md at main · badlogic/pi-mono (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/quickstart.md)\n\n### Pi Packages [...] Bundle and share extensions, skills, prompts, and themes via npm or git. Find packages on npmjs.com or Discord. [...] > **Security:** Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions execute arbitrary code, and skills can instruct the model to perform any action including running executables. Review source code before installing third-party packages. [...] ```bash pi install npm:@foo/pi-tools pi install npm:@foo/pi-tools@1.2.3 # pinned version pi install git:github.com/u\nSource: packages/coding-agent/README.md at 83378aad · badlogic/pi-mono (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/83378aad/packages/coding-agent/README.md)\n\n> **Deprecated.** This package has been superseded by **pi-guard**. > pi-guard has a superset of the same features plus broader threat detection (forbidden paths, environment variable inspection, etc.). > No further updates will be published for pi-unbash. Please migrate to pi-guard. [...] A high-security, AST-powered bash confirmation extension for the `pi` coding agent. [...] **`pi-unbash` is different.** It uses `unbash`, a fast, zero-dependency TypeScript parser that generates a full POSIX-c\nSource: jdiamond/pi-unbash (https://github.com/jdiamond/pi-unbash)\n\n---\n\n**Sources:**\n1. packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md at main · earendil-works/pi\n   https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md\n\n2. @mariozechner/pi-coding-agent\n   https://registry.npmjs.org/@mariozechner/pi-coding-agent\n\n3. Pi Packages - Pi Coding Agent\n   https://pi.dev/docs/latest/packages\n\n4. Pi Coding Agent | Ry Walker Research | Ry Walker\n   https://rywalker.com/research/pi\n\n5. open-pi-coding-agent\n   https://registry.npmjs.org/open-pi-coding-agent\n\n6. packages/coding-agent/docs/quickstart.md at main · badlogic/pi-mono\n   https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/quickstart.md\n\n7. packages/coding-agent/README.md at 83378aad · badlogic/pi-mono\n   https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/83378aad/packages/coding-agent/README.md\n\n8. jdiamond/pi-unbash\n   https://github.com/jdiamond/pi-unbash\n\n## Query: \"\"pi install\" \"pinned\" \"package filtering\" \"Pi Coding Agent\" security\"\n\n> **Security:** Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions execute arbitrary code, and skills can instruct the model to perform any action including running executables. Review source code before installing third-party packages. [...] ```bash pi install npm:@foo/bar@1.0.0 pi install git:github.com/user/repo@v1 pi install https://github.com/user/repo # raw URLs work too pi install /absolute/path/to/package pi install ./relative/path/to/package pi remove npm:@foo/bar pi list # show instal\nSource: packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md at main · earendil-works/pi (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md)\n\n> Installing and creating Pi packages to share extensions, skills, prompts, and themes via npm or git [...] Pi can help you create pi packages. Just ask it to bundle your extensions, skills, prompt templates, or themes. [...] Pi packages bundle extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes so you can share them through npm or git. A package can declare resources in `package.json` under the `pi` key, or use conventional directories. [...] Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions exe\nSource: Pi Packages - Pi (https://www.mintlify.com/badlogic/pi-mono/coding-agent/pi-packages)\n\n### Package Commands [...] ```bash pi install <source> [-l] # Install package, -l for project-local pi remove <source> [-l] # Remove package pi uninstall <source> [-l] # Alias for remove pi update [source|self|pi] # Update pi and packages; skips pinned packages pi update --extensions # Update packages only pi update --self # Update pi only pi update --extension <src> # Update one package pi list # List installed packages pi config # Enable/disable package resources ``` [...] See Pi Packages for \nSource: packages/coding-agent/docs/usage.md at main · earendil-works/pi (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/usage.md)\n\nPi supports installing packages that provide extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes. [...] Pi supports three types of package sources: [...] 1. **npm**: `npm:package-name` or `npm:@org/package` (optionally `@version`) 2. **git**: `git:host/owner/repo` or just `https://github.com/owner/repo` (optionally `@ref`) 3. **local**: path to a directory (e.g. `../my-package`) [...] Install a package globally (user scope): [...] ```bash pi install npm:pi-skills pi install git:github.com/someuser/\nSource: docs/packages.md at main · Dicklesworthstone/pi_agent_rust (https://github.com/Dicklesworthstone/pi_agent_rust/blob/main/docs/packages.md)\n\nPi ships with powerful defaults but skips features like sub agents and plan mode. Instead, you can ask pi to build what you want [...] package that matches your workflow. [...] ### Pi Packages [...] Bundle and share extensions, skills, prompts, and themes via npm or git. Find packages on npmjs.com or Discord. [...] Security: Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions execute arbitrary code, and skills can instruct the model to perform any action including running executables. Review sou\nSource: pi-mono/packages/coding-agent at main · badlogic/pi-mono · GitHub (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/tree/main/packages/coding-agent)\n\nall projects) | [...] directory) | [...] | Setting | Type | Default | Description | |---------|------|---------|-------------| | `theme` | string | `\"dark\"` | Theme name (`\"dark\"`, `\"light\"`, or custom) | | `quietStartup` | boolean | `false` | Hide startup header | | `collapseChangelog` | boolean | `false` | Show condensed changelog after updates | | `enableInstallTelemetry` | boolean | `true` | Send an anonymous install/update version ping after first install or changelog-detected updates. This\nSource: packages/coding-agent/docs/settings.md at main · earendil-works/pi (https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/settings.md)\n\n/update telemetry [...] changelog- [...] update, sends an anonymous version ping to `https://pi.dev/api/report-install`. Opt [...] by setting `enable [...] Telemetry` to `false` in `settings [...] `, or by [...] disable update checks; Pi may still contact ` [...] latest version unless update checks are disabled or offline mode is enabled. [...] ### Pi Packages [...] and share extensions, skills, prompts, [...] npm or git. [...] npmjs.com or Discord. [...] > **Security:** Pi packages run with ful\nSource: heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md at refs · badlogic/pi-mono (https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/refs/heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md)\n\n### Pi Packages [...] Bundle and share extensions, skills, prompts, and themes via npm or git. Find packages on npmjs.com or Discord. [...] > **Security:** Pi packages run with full system access. Extensions execute arbitrary code, and skills can instruct the model to perform any action including running executables. Review source code before installing third-party packages. [...] ```bash pi install npm:@foo/pi-tools pi install npm:@foo/pi-tools@1.2.3 # pinned version pi install git:github.com/u\nSource: open-pi-coding-agent (https://registry.npmjs.org/open-pi-coding-agent)\n\n---\n\n**Sources:**\n1. packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md at main · earendil-works/pi\n   https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/packages.md\n\n2. Pi Packages - Pi\n   https://www.mintlify.com/badlogic/pi-mono/coding-agent/pi-packages\n\n3. packages/coding-agent/docs/usage.md at main · earendil-works/pi\n   https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/usage.md\n\n4. docs/packages.md at main · Dicklesworthstone/pi_agent_rust\n   https://github.com/Dicklesworthstone/pi_agent_rust/blob/main/docs/packages.md\n\n5. pi-mono/packages/coding-agent at main · badlogic/pi-mono · GitHub\n   https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/tree/main/packages/coding-agent\n\n6. packages/coding-agent/docs/settings.md at main · earendil-works/pi\n   https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/settings.md\n\n7. heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md at refs · badlogic/pi-mono\n   https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/refs/heads/main/packages/coding-agent/README.md\n\n8. open-pi-coding-agent\n   https://registry.npmjs.org/open-pi-coding-agent\n\n## Query: \"\"pi-agent-browser-native\" security redacts secrets agent-browser Pi extension\"\n\nA Pi extension that lets coding agents drive real browser sessions with a native `agent_browser` tool instead of brittle shell commands. [...] It is for Pi users who want agents to browse sites, inspect pages, click through flows, capture screenshots, use persistent profiles, and handle authenticated web apps without spending context on `agent-browser` CLI ceremony. [...] - shell strings are easy for agents to quote wrong - large page snapshots can waste model context - screenshots and downloads\nSource: fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native (https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native)\n\n- Repository: fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native | pi extension that exposes agent-browser as a native tool for browser automation | 2 stars | TypeScript - Branch: main [...] Native `pi` integration for `agent-browser`. [...] The package scaffold, native `agent_browser` tool, local typecheck/test setup, and release/package verification workflow are in place. [...] Expose `agent-browser` to `pi` as a native tool so agents can automate the browser without going through a bash-backed skill. [...] -\nSource: README.md at main · fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native (https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native/blob/main/README.md)\n\n- plain-text inspection commands like`agent_browser --help` and`--version` now stay stateless: they no longer claim the implicit managed session or leave behind ambiguous`parseError` details on success [...] - extension-managed session ownership is now reconstructed from persisted tool details on resume/reload while still preserving cwd-hash isolation across same-named checkouts and worktrees [...] - echoed tool updates/details now redact sensitive invocation values and structured secret-bearing\nSource: Releases · fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native · GitHub (https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native/releases)\n\n# pi-agent-browser-native [...] Native`pi` integration for agent-browser. [...] The package scaffold, native`agent_browser` tool, local typecheck/test setup, and release/package verification workflow are in place. [...] Expose`agent-browser` to`pi` as a native tool so agents can automate the browser without going through a bash-backed skill. [...] - Not bundled: users install`agent-browser` separately and keep it on`PATH` - Latest-version only: no backward-compatibility support or shims for olde\nSource: GitHub - fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native at v0.2.12 · GitHub (https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native/tree/v0.2.12)\n\n- Repository: fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native | pi extension that exposes agent-browser as a native tool for browser automation | 3 stars | TypeScript - Branch: main [...] Build this as a **thin `pi` extension/package** that exposes `agent-browser` as one native tool while keeping upstream `agent-browser` as the source of truth. [...] The package install path is the primary product path. Local checkout development should use explicit CLI loading, while package-manifest behavior and packaged c\nSource: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md at main · fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native (https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md)\n\npi-agent-browser-native 0.2.10 on npm - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software [...] pi-agent-browser-native [...] pi extension that exposes agent-browser as a native tool for browser automation [...] # pi-agent-browser-native [...] Native`pi` integration for agent-browser. [...] The package scaffold, native`agent_browser` tool, local typecheck/test setup, and release/package verification workflow are in place. [...] Expose`agent-browser` to`pi` as a native tool so a\nSource: pi-agent-browser-native 0.2.10 on npm - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software (https://libraries.io/npm/pi-agent-browser-native)\n\n- Repository: fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native | pi extension that exposes agent-browser as a native tool for browser automation | 3 stars | TypeScript - Branch: main [...] ```md # pi-agent-browser [...] Dedicated `pi` integration of `agent-browser` as a native tool. [...] - Do **not** bundle `agent-browser` in this project. - Assume `agent-browser` is installed separately and available on `PATH`. - Target the current locally installed `agent-browser` version only. - Do **not** add backwards-c\nSource: AGENTS.md at main · fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native (https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native/blob/main/AGENTS.md)\n\n- Repository: fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native | pi extension that exposes agent-browser as a native tool for browser automation | 3 stars | TypeScript - Branch: main [...] the product requirements and constraints for `pi-agent-browser-native`. [...] - `agent-browser` is an external dependency. - This project does **not** bundle `agent-browser`. - Users install `agent-browser` separately and keep it available on `PATH`. - User-facing install guidance should point to the upstream `agent-browser\nSource: docs/REQUIREMENTS.md at main · fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native (https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native/blob/main/docs/REQUIREMENTS.md)\n\n---\n\n**Sources:**\n1. fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native\n   https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native\n\n2. README.md at main · fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native\n   https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native/blob/main/README.md\n\n3. Releases · fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native · GitHub\n   https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native/releases\n\n4. GitHub - fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native at v0.2.12 · GitHub\n   https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native/tree/v0.2.12\n\n5. docs/ARCHITECTURE.md at main · fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native\n   https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md\n\n6. pi-agent-browser-native 0.2.10 on npm - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software\n   https://libraries.io/npm/pi-agent-browser-native\n\n7. AGENTS.md at main · fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native\n   https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native/blob/main/AGENTS.md\n\n8. docs/REQUIREMENTS.md at main · fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native\n   https://github.com/fitchmultz/pi-agent-browser-native/blob/main/docs/REQUIREMENTS.md\n\n## Query: \"\"pi-lens\" auto-install tools intercept write edit Pi security risk\"\n\nOn every `write` and `edit`, pi-lens runs a fast, language-aware pipeline (checks depend on file language, project config, and installed tools): [...] 1. **Secrets scan** — blocking; aborts the write if credentials are detected 2. **Auto-format** — deferred to `agent_end` by default; queued files are formatted once after all agent tool calls complete. Use `--immediate-format` for per-edit formatting 3. **Auto-fix** — safe autofixes from 6 tools (Biome `check --write`, Ruff `check --fix`, ESLint \nSource: pi-lens (https://registry.npmjs.org/pi-lens)\n\n/** * Auto-Installation System for pi-lens * * Minimal auto-install: Core tools that run frequently. * Other tools require manual installation with clear instructions. * * Auto-install (8 tools): * - typescript-language-server (TypeScript LSP) * - pyright (Python LSP) * - ruff (Python linting) * - @biomejs/biome (JS/TS/JSON linting/formatting) * - madge (circular dependency detection) * - jscpd (duplicate code detection) * - @ast-grep/cli (structural code search) * - knip (dead code detection) *\nSource: Index.ts (https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pi-lens@3.6.5/clients/installer/index.ts)\n\n### On Write/Edit [...] On every `write` and `edit`, pi-lens runs a fast, language-aware pipeline (checks depend on file language, project config, and installed tools): [...] - **Formatting + autofix**: language/tool-specific formatters and safe autofixers (Biome, Ruff, ESLint, and other toolchain-native formatters when available) - **Type checking**: unified LSP (enabled by default) with language fallbacks (for example `ts-lsp`, `pyright`) - **Lint + static analysis**: active runners for the cu\nSource: ricardoraposo/pi-lens (https://github.com/ricardoraposo/pi-lens)\n\nOn every `write` and `edit`, pi-lens runs a fast, language-aware pipeline (checks depend on file language, project config, and installed tools): [...] 1. **Secrets scan** — blocking; aborts the write if credentials are detected 2. **Auto-format** — deferred to `agent_end` by default; queued files are formatted once after all agent tool calls complete. Use `--immediate-format` for per-edit formatting 3. **Auto-fix** — safe autofixes from 6 tools (Biome `check --write`, Ruff `check --fix`, ESLint \nSource: README.md at master · apmantza/pi-lens (https://github.com/apmantza/pi-lens/blob/master/README.md)\n\nOn every `write` and `edit`, pi-lens runs a fast, language-aware pipeline (checks depend on file language, project config, and installed tools): [...] 1. **Secrets scan** — blocking; aborts the write if credentials are detected 2. **Auto-format** — 26 language-specific formatters (Biome, Prettier, Ruff, gofmt, rustfmt, and 21 others) 3. **Auto-fix** — safe autofixes from 6 tools (Biome `check --write`, Ruff `check --fix`, ESLint `--fix`, stylelint `--fix`, sqlfluff `fix`, RuboCop `-a`) applied b\nSource: apmantza/pi-lens (https://github.com/apmantza/pi-lens/)\n\n### On Write/Edit [...] On every `write` and `edit`, pi-lens runs a fast, language-aware pipeline (checks depend on file language, project config, and installed tools): [...] - **Formatting + autofix**: language/tool-specific formatters and safe autofixers (Biome, Ruff, ESLint, and other toolchain-native formatters when available) - **Type checking**: unified LSP (enabled by default) with language fallbacks (for example `ts-lsp`, `pyright`) - **Lint + static analysis**: active runners for the cu\nSource: shayshahal/pi-lens (https://github.com/shayshahal/pi-lens)\n\nOn every `write` and `edit`, pi-lens runs a fast, language-aware pipeline (checks depend on file language, project config, and installed tools): [...] 1. **Secrets scan** — blocking; aborts the write if credentials are detected 2. **Auto-format** — language-specific formatters (Biome, Prettier, Ruff, gofmt, rustfmt, and 25+ others) 3. **Auto-fix** — safe autofixes from 6 tools (Biome `--write`, Ruff `--fix`, ESLint `--fix`, stylelint `--fix`, sqlfluff `fix`, RuboCop `-a`) applied before analysis\nSource: Roman-Galeev/pi-lens (https://github.com/Roman-Galeev/pi-lens)\n\nOn every `write` and `edit`, pi-lens runs a fast, language-aware pipeline (checks depend on file language, project config, and installed tools): [...] - **Formatting + autofix**: language/tool-specific formatters and safe autofixers (Biome, Ruff, ESLint, and other toolchain-native formatters when available) - **Type checking**: unified LSP (enabled by default) with language fallbacks (for example `ts-lsp`, `pyright`) - **Lint + static analysis**: active runners for the current language and confi\nSource: anh-chu/pi-lens (https://github.com/anh-chu/pi-lens)\n\n---\n\n**Sources:**\n1. pi-lens\n   https://registry.npmjs.org/pi-lens\n\n2. Index.ts\n   https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pi-lens@3.6.5/clients/installer/index.ts\n\n3. ricardoraposo/pi-lens\n   https://github.com/ricardoraposo/pi-lens\n\n4. README.md at master · apmantza/pi-lens\n   https://github.com/apmantza/pi-lens/blob/master/README.md\n\n5. apmantza/pi-lens\n   https://github.com/apmantza/pi-lens/\n\n6. shayshahal/pi-lens\n   https://github.com/shayshahal/pi-lens\n\n7. Roman-Galeev/pi-lens\n   https://github.com/Roman-Galeev/pi-lens\n\n8. anh-chu/pi-lens\n   https://github.com/an